window.__MT_SEARCH=[{"id":"aidoc-alerts","name":"Aidoc Alerts","brief":"Watches the Aidoc widget and raises an orange alert when Aidoc has a positive finding that's actually relevant to the study you have open — irrelevant flags are filtered out.","category":"Worklist & Productivity","tags":"aidoc ai detection pe ich orange border relevance filter","body":"What it does Scrapes the Aidoc shortcut widget in the background and shows an orange border alert on the notification box when Aidoc has detected a positive finding for the current study. Findings are checked for study-type relevance before alerting, so an Aidoc flag that can't apply to your open study doesn't nag you. How to turn it on / set it up 1. Check Aidoc AI detection in Settings → Alerts → Alert Triggers . 2. Required Aidoc configuration: in the Aidoc widget, set Contextual summary display → No animation . The animated display defeats the scrape; the settings page has a ? button showing exactly where this option lives. Aidoc itself must be installed and running on the workstation — MosaicTools only reads its widget, it doesn't talk to Aidoc's servers. Using it Nothing to do during a shift: when you open a study that Aidoc has flagged positive the red-dot indicator on a finding icon , the orange alert appears so you know to look for it before you start dictating. Negative/absent findings produce no alert. Tips & gotchas - The relevance filter is the point: Aidoc's widget shows results for the patient , which may include findings from a different exam. MosaicTools verifies the finding type plausibly belongs to your open study's type before alerting. - Positive/negative status is read from the widget's colored indicator dots, so unusual display scaling or a themed/updated Aidoc widget can break detection — if alerts stop, check the widget still looks normal and animation is still off. - The Aidoc scrape runs independently of the clinical-history display, so it keeps working even with the notification box in alerts-only mode."},{"id":"auto-measure","name":"Auto Measure","brief":"Point at a lesion in InteleViewer, press a button, and get an automatic bidimensional measurement drawn over the image — ready to paste into the report.","category":"Measurement & Imaging","tags":"measurement lesion segmentation SAM MobileSAM bidimensional overlay InteleViewer automeasure","body":"What it does Auto Measure captures the region of the InteleViewer image around your cursor, segments the lesion under it, and draws a bidimensional measurement overlay long axis + perpendicular short axis, with the numbers right on top of the image. Segmentation runs two ways at once: a MobileSAM neural model on your GPU via DirectML plus an adaptive region-growing heuristic with edge-snap refinement — so it handles both well-circumscribed and messier lesions. Calibration is ruler-free: measurements derive from the workstation's monitor pitch and the true-size zoom level, so you don't need the on-screen ruler overlay for it to be accurate. How to turn it on / set it up Map the Auto Measure action to a PowerMic/SpeechMike button or hotkey under Settings → Keys & Buttons. There's no separate enable toggle — the action does the work. Using it 1. Hover your cursor over the lesion in InteleViewer. 2. Trigger Auto Measure. 3. The measurement overlay appears over the lesion and auto-dismisses after about 5 seconds click to dismiss early . Focus returns to InteleViewer the moment the measurement appears, so your tool-select mouse button keeps working — no need to click the image first. 4. The measurement is available to paste into the report. The first measurement after launch pays a GPU cold-start cost ~2 s for kernel compilation ; subsequent measurements come back in a few hundred milliseconds on a modern GPU. Tips & gotchas - It measures what it can segment — a lesion with almost no contrast against its background may get a poor boundary. Always eyeball the overlay before dictating the number. - Works with InteleViewer rulers in either yellow or orange used only for legacy calibration; the pitch-based calibration is the default . - A side-by-side compare popup showing the SAM crop vs. the heuristic crop exists behind a developer flag SamCompareEnabled — it is not a user-facing setting."},{"id":"auto-process","name":"Auto-Process on Open","brief":"Runs Process Report automatically the moment a drafted or capture study opens, so AI-drafted findings land in the Final Report before you've touched anything.","category":"Worklist & Productivity","tags":"auto process drafted capture tech worksheet process report inpatient drafted","body":"What it does For hands-off study types, MosaicTools presses Process Report for you at study open: - Auto-process drafted studies on open — when a study arrives with an AI preliminary draft, Process Report runs immediately so the drafted findings are placed into the Final Report with no manual step and drafts that arrive with findings still unprocessed get fixed . - Auto-process capture studies on open — same idea for CAPTURE studies e.g. ultrasound tech-worksheet OCR , independent of the drafted toggle. - Auto-process all studies after macro insertion — runs Process Report once on every new study, but only after all of the study's opening macros have landed in the transcript. For workflows that insert a template macro at open and want it processed straight away. How to turn it on / set it up All three toggles live in Settings → Behavior under Report Processing. Enable whichever match your workflow; they're independent of each other. Related: Ignore Inpatient Drafted select all after auto-insertions in the Inpatient XR Handling section auto-selects all text on inpatient X-rays after macro/clinical-history insertions, with a choice of All Inpatient XR or Inpatient Chest XR only — useful when you re-dictate those from scratch. Using it Open a drafted study; by the time you've looked at the images the findings are already in the Final Report and the impression is ready for your attention. It fires once per study open. Tips & gotchas - Auto-process is skipped while you're actively dictating — it will not fire on studies you're talking into, only genuinely hands-off ones. It also skips addendums macro-triggered variant . - The macro variant waits for all matching opening macros before processing, whether a study matches zero, one, or several. - Combine with Auto-Stop/Auto-Restart Dictation and the sign warning for a fully guarded flow."},{"id":"capture-series-image","name":"Capture Series/Image","brief":"OCRs the InteleViewer viewport header to grab the current series and image numbers and inserts them into your report, formatted your way.","category":"Measurement & Imaging","tags":"series image number OCR capture reference InteleViewer se im","body":"What it does When you want to reference a finding by series and image \"series 4, image 52\" , this action reads those numbers off the screen for you: it screenshots the corner of the active InteleViewer viewport, runs OCR on the header text, extracts the series and image numbers, and inserts them into the Mosaic report at your cursor — formatted through your template. How to turn it on / set it up 1. Map the Capture Series/Image action to a mic button or hotkey under Settings → Keys & Buttons. 2. Optionally customize the output under Settings → Text Templates → Series/Image , using the {series} and {image} placeholders e.g. series {series}, image {image} . Using it 1. Have the image you want to reference up in InteleViewer. 2. Trigger the action. MosaicTools locates the active viewport it looks for InteleViewer's yellow active-viewport marker; if none is found it captures around your mouse cursor instead . 3. A \"Capturing...\" toast appears, then \"Inserted: …\" with the formatted text — which lands in the report editor without you touching the keyboard. With CDP enabled the insertion goes straight into the editor DOM; without it, Mosaic is focused first. Tips & gotchas - OCR needs the viewport header text to be legible — if the overlay text is disabled or the capture misses, you'll get a \"Could not extract series/image\" toast; click into the viewport so the yellow marker is where you expect and try again. - The action is blocked while an addendum is open \"Cannot paste into addendum\" , since inserting there would edit the wrong document. - The fallback capture region is centered on your mouse — if the yellow box isn't detected, park the cursor near the series/image text before triggering."},{"id":"cdp-integration","name":"CDP Integration","brief":"Reads Mosaic and Clario through the Chrome DevTools Protocol instead of screen-level UI Automation — faster, more reliable, and the foundation for the in-editor visual features.","category":"Setup & Platform","tags":"cdp chrome devtools protocol dom scraping clario cdp webview automation","body":"What it does Mosaic's report screen is a web page inside an embedded browser, and Clario is a Chrome web app. CDP lets MosaicTools read and modify those pages directly through the browser's own debugging protocol — instant JS evaluation instead of walking accessibility trees. That means faster and more accurate scraping accession, drafted state, clinical history, exam notes, Priority/Class, critical note creation , plus a set of features that are only possible with DOM access: independent column scrolling, auto-scroll to the cursor while dictating, dictated-text highlighting, visual report enhancements, flashing alert text, in-editor impression-fixer buttons, MTRec boxes, and Mosaic zoom memory. How to turn it on / set it up Mosaic — in Settings → Experimental → Mosaic CDP Direct DOM Access , check Use CDP for Mosaic interaction requires a Mosaic restart the first time , then enable whichever sub-features you want. Clario — in the Clario CDP Direct DOM Access section, check Use CDP for Clario interaction , enter your Clario URL , and click Setup Chrome CDP Shortcut . This creates a \"Clario CDP\" desktop shortcut and a profile junction so Chrome launches with CDP enabled while keeping your normal profile. Close Chrome before first launching it, then always open Clario from that shortcut. The CDP Status dashboard at the bottom of the section shows live green/red dots for both connections. Using it Invisible when working: everything is just faster and steadier. The Clario TBWU injection and instant critical-note creation also depend on it. Tips & gotchas - UI Automation remains the fallback — if CDP is unavailable, MosaicTools silently falls back, so enabling CDP can't leave you worse off. - If CDP-dependent features stop, check the status dashboard first; the Clario side usually means Chrome wasn't launched from the \"Clario CDP\" shortcut. - Known interaction: the Independent Column Scrolling CSS can corrupt the page for RadPair users — turn that sub-option off if you r"},{"id":"clinical-history-box","name":"Clinical History / Notification Box","brief":"A floating box showing the current study's clinical history — cleaned up, with age/sex added and auto-pasted into the report — that doubles as the surface where all MosaicTools alerts appear.","category":"Priors & Clinical History","tags":"clinical history notification box alerts age sex drafted indicator auto-paste floating window","body":"What it does A small floating window that follows your worklist: for each study it shows the clinical history , the patient's age and sex , and a green Drafted indicator border when the report already has an IMPRESSION a draft in progress . It can also fix the history for you — when malformed history text is detected, MosaicTools auto-pastes a corrected version into the report, optionally prepending the patient's age \"65-year-old\" and sex. The same box is where MosaicTools' alerts appear: its border changes color per trigger — pulsing yellow for patient mismatch, red for template mismatch, flashing red for gender check, pulsing orange for findings/impression mismatch, pulsing red for measurement & laterality consistency, orange for Aidoc detections, purple for stroke studies. Each alert has its own page; this box is where you see them. How to turn it on / set it up Settings → Alerts → Notification Box: - Enable Notification Box — the master toggle requires Mosaic scraping to be on . - Show clinical history in notification box — uncheck for alerts-only mode: the box stays hidden until an alert fires. - Hide when no study open — hides it between studies. - Auto-paste corrected history — pastes the corrected clinical history into Mosaic when malformed text is detected. - Add patient age / Add patient sex — prepend these when auto-pasting. - Show Drafted indicator — the green border for draft-in-progress studies. Using it There's nothing to trigger — it updates automatically as the background scrape follows your current study. Glance at it as each case opens: history, age/sex, drafted or not, and any alert color. Tips & gotchas - The stroke alert is clickable: with \"Click alert to create Clario note\" on, clicking the purple alert creates the Clario communication note. - Alerts-only mode is popular with people who feel the box is one window too many — you keep the safety net without the always-on display. - If the box shows nothing on studies with hyphenated accession nu"},{"id":"connectivity-monitor","name":"Connectivity Monitor","brief":"Four tiny status dots on the bar — Mirth, Mosaic, Clario, InteleViewer — so when things feel slow you can see in one glance whether it's the network or you. In development — not functional yet.","category":"Setup & Platform","tags":"network connectivity status dots mirth servers offline vpn","body":"⚠️ In development. This feature is not functional yet — the toggle exists in Settings but the monitoring is still being built and validated. Don't rely on the dots; this page describes the intended behavior. What it does Periodically checks reachability of the four servers your reading workflow depends on and shows the results as a 2×2 grid of colored dots on the widget bar: Mirth, Mosaic on the top row, Clario, InteleViewer on the bottom. Green is healthy; amber/orange/red indicate slow, degraded, or offline; gray is unknown. Clicking the dots opens a details window with per-server status. How to turn it on / set it up In Settings → Experimental → Network Monitor : 1. Check Show connectivity status dots . 2. Optionally tune Check every 10–120 sec, default 30 and Timeout 1–10 sec, default 5 . Using it Ignore it until something feels wrong. Dictation lagging, worklist frozen, priors not loading? Glance at the dots: all green means the servers answer and the problem is elsewhere Mosaic itself, your session ; a red dot names the culprit before you've opened a ticket. Click for the details popup with each server's current state. Tips & gotchas - This is a reachability check, not an application health check — a server can answer pings while its application layer is struggling. Treat green as \"network path is fine\", not \"everything is fine\". - It lives under Experimental deliberately; the interval and timeout are conservative so the checks themselves never load the network. - Remote/VPN readers get the most value: a flapping VPN shows up as synchronized dot changes across all four servers."},{"id":"consistency-checker","name":"Measurement & Laterality Consistency","brief":"Flags a pulsing red alert when a measurement or a left/right differs between FINDINGS and IMPRESSION, or when the report's laterality contradicts the study description.","category":"Report Processing & QA","tags":"consistency measurement laterality left right wrong side mismatch qa alerts","body":"What it does Three checks run automatically on the drafted report and surface as a pulsing red alert on the notification box: 1. Measurement consistency — the same finding carries different sizes in FINDINGS vs IMPRESSION a 4 mm calculus that became 5 mm in the impression . 2. Laterality consistency — a structure is left in FINDINGS and right in IMPRESSION or vice versa . 3. Study description laterality — the report says \"right metacarpal\" but the study is a LEFT HAND. Suppressed for studies with bilateral components AP pelvis, chest, etc. , and spine levels like L4/L5 are never mistaken for laterality. How to turn it on / set it up Settings → Alerts → Alert Triggers → Measurement & laterality consistency. The notification box itself must be enabled Enable Notification Box , same tab — the checker's alerts appear even in alerts-only mode. Using it You'll usually see it right after Process Report, once both sections exist. The alert names the discrepancy \"calculus: 4mm→5mm\", \"kidney: left→right\" , and when the CDP connection is active the mismatched text flashes in the report editor itself so you can find it without hunting. Click the alert to dismiss it; dismissals are tracked per-mismatch and cleared when you change studies, so fixing one issue won't re-fire it but a new one still will. Tips & gotchas - Measurement matching is one-to-one, so two findings that share anatomy keywords two renal cysts of different sizes don't cross-trigger false positives. - It compares FINDINGS against IMPRESSION — it can't know which of the two numbers is the correct one; that's your call. - This is a different check from the Findings/Impression mismatch alert, which catches contradictions of assertion \"no obstruction\" vs \"obstruction\" rather than numbers and sides."},{"id":"critical-findings","name":"Critical Findings","brief":"One button press scrapes the Clario exam note, formats the critical-communication details (who, when), and pastes a ready-made documentation line into your Mosaic report.","category":"Worklist & Productivity","tags":"critical clario exam note communication paste documentation","body":"What it does When a study has a critical-communication note in Clario, this action reads the exam note directly from Clario, extracts the contact name and time, formats them into your documentation template, and pastes the result into the Mosaic report — no window switching, no retyping names and timestamps. How to turn it on / set it up 1. Map the Critical Findings action to a mic button or hotkey in Settings → Keys & Buttons → Open Keys Configuration... 2. Customize the output in Settings → Text & Templates → Templates → Critical Findings . The template supports the placeholders {name} , {time} , and {date} . 3. In Settings → Profile , set Doctor Name to your name as it appears in Clario — this filters your own notes out of the results so the pasted contact is the person you spoke to, not you. 4. Optional: the Timezone setting in Profile converts note times to a specific timezone; Keep original preserves whatever the note says. Using it Open the study in Mosaic, press your mapped button, and the formatted line appears at the cursor. If MosaicTools created the Clario note itself see Critical Notes , the study is also added to the session's critical-studies tracker. Tips & gotchas - The scrape needs the study's exam note to actually exist in Clario; on a study with no note you'll get nothing to paste. - Debugging: hold the Win key while triggering the action to see the raw scraped data in a dialog. There's also right-click the bar → Debug → Copy Critical Findings Debug . - Works via Clario CDP when configured instant JS reads with UI Automation as the fallback."},{"id":"critical-notes","name":"Critical Notes","brief":"Create a Clario communication note without leaving Mosaic — including a one-press \"Create Negative Critical\" that documents a negative stroke read using Clario's canned message.","category":"Worklist & Productivity","tags":"critical note clario communication note stroke negative stroke create critical","body":"What it does Two mappable actions drive Clario's communication-note workflow for you: - Create Critical Note — opens and submits a Clario communication note for the current study the classic use case: documenting a stroke or other critical result call . - Create Negative Critical — the same flow, but it first selects the \"Negative STROKE results\" canned message from Clario's Default Note Messages field before submitting, documenting a negative stroke read in one press. How to turn it on / set it up Map Create Critical Note and/or Create Negative Critical to a mic button or hotkey in Settings → Keys & Buttons → Open Keys Configuration... No other setup is required, though the automation works best with Clario CDP configured Settings → Experimental → Clario CDP . Using it With the study open, press the mapped button. MosaicTools drives Clario's note dialog, submits it, and shows a toast. If Track critical studies this session is enabled Settings → Alerts , the study is added to the ⚠ badge on the bar so you can find it again later. Tips & gotchas - Negative criticals suppress the automatic stroke note : if you've already created a negative critical on a study, the stroke-detection auto-note won't also fire on Process Report — you won't end up with contradictory notes. - Create Negative Critical was added in 5.5.0; make sure the \"Negative STROKE results\" canned message exists in your Clario Default Note Messages, since the action selects it by name. - These actions pair naturally with Stroke Detection, which can create the positive note for you automatically or on a click of the purple alert."},{"id":"critical-studies-tracker","name":"Critical Studies Tracker","brief":"A session-long list of every study you placed a critical note on, behind a red ⚠ count on the bar — double-click any entry to reopen that study in Clario.","category":"Worklist & Productivity","tags":"critical studies badge session list reopen in clario warning triangle","body":"What it does Keeps a running list of the critical studies you've placed notes on this session. A bright red ⚠ with a count appears on the MosaicTools bar; clicking it opens a popup listing each study patient, study, time . It answers the end-of-shift question \"which criticals did I call tonight, and did I close the loop on all of them?\" How to turn it on / set it up Check Track critical studies this session in Settings → Alerts → Critical Studies Tracker . Entries are added automatically whenever a critical note is created through MosaicTools Create Critical Note, Create Negative Critical, stroke auto-note . Using it - The ⚠ badge and count appear on the bar as soon as the first critical note is placed. Hover for the tooltip \"Critical notes placed this session — click to view\" . - Click the badge to open the list. - Double-click an entry to reopen that study in Clario — MosaicTools writes a small XML file into the folder Clario watches, and Clario opens the study. No searching the worklist. - Hover an entry and click its X to remove it from the list e.g. once you've documented the callback . Tips & gotchas - The list is session-only and memory-only — it is never written to disk, and it clears when MosaicTools exits. Treat it as a working checklist, not a permanent record. - When Mosaic doesn't provide a patient name for an entry, the tracker falls back to the study you're viewing in InteleViewer rather than showing \"Unknown\". - The double-click-to-open trick relies on the Clario XML watch folder existing on your workstation standard on Fluency for Imaging installs ; if Clario doesn't react, that folder may not be configured on your box."},{"id":"custom-process-report","name":"Custom Process Report","brief":"An experimental alternate to Process Report where MosaicTools itself calls an LLM of your choice — Gemini, OpenAI, Groq, or xAI Grok, or all four racing in Quad Compare — instead of Mosaic's built-in one.","category":"Report Processing & QA","tags":"llm gemini openai gpt groq grok quad compare experimental custom processing","body":"What it does Normal Process Report hands your dictation to Mosaic's own LLM. Custom Process Report bypasses that entirely: MosaicTools reads the transcript and template directly via the Chrome DevTools Protocol connection to Mosaic , sends them to a cloud LLM you configure, and writes the merged report back. It lives under the \"Beyond Experimental\" divider for a reason — it works, but it replaces a core Mosaic function, so treat it as a power-user experiment. How to turn it on / set it up 1. Settings → Experimental , scroll to the BEYOND EXPERIMENTAL section, and check Custom Process Report LLM . CDP must be enabled also on the Experimental tab . 2. Pick a Provider : Google Gemini, OpenAI GPT, Groq, xAI Grok, or Quad Compare — which races one model from each of the four providers and uses the result you prefer. Each provider needs its own API key \"Get Key\" buttons link to the right console ; model pickers show per-million-token pricing. 3. Map Custom Process Report to a key or button under Keys & Buttons — it does not replace your normal Process Report mapping. Using it Press your Custom Process Report key instead of or alongside the regular one. The transcript and template go to your chosen LLM and the structured report is written back into Mosaic. Tips & gotchas - These are paid cloud APIs billed to your own keys — costs are small per report, but they're yours. - PHI is scrubbed before anything is sent. - Keep regular Process Report mapped too: if CDP is down or the provider errors, the built-in path is the fallback. - MTRec boxes and the other post-process surfaces are built around the standard Process Report flow; the custom path is primarily about the report text itself."},{"id":"custom-stt","name":"Custom STT Mode","brief":"Replaces Mosaic's built-in speech recognition with MosaicTools' own multi-engine dictation pipeline — faster, more accurate on radiology vocabulary, and entirely under your control.","category":"Dictation & Speech","tags":"stt speech dictation speech-to-text custom dictation voice recognition","body":"What it does Custom STT Mode takes over dictation from Mosaic's built-in speech recognition. Your microphone audio goes to one or usually several speech engines in parallel, the results are merged, run through radiology-aware text processing units, spine levels, punctuation, your own word replacements , and pasted into the Mosaic report. You keep your normal workflow — Record button, transcript, Process Report — but the recognition underneath is yours to choose and tune. How to turn it on / set it up 1. Open Settings → Speech-to-Text and check Enable Custom STT Mode . 2. Click ⚡ Quick Setup Wizard — it walks you through creating the engine accounts and API keys step by step, then assembles the recommended ensemble for you. 3. When Mosaic shows its WebHID device prompt, cancel it MosaicTools handles the microphone . The wizard also helps turn off Mosaic's \"Auto Transcript Record\" so Mosaic doesn't grab the mic first. Cost honesty: the cloud engines are paid, on your own account — typically fractions of a cent per minute of dictation, pay-per-use. The on-device medASR engine is free. Using it Dictate exactly as before. Text appears near-instantly see Turbo Paste and quietly refines as the other engines weigh in. A floating indicator shows live transcription, beeps confirm start/stop, and Auto-start on case open can begin recording automatically when a new study opens stopping on Sign Report . Tips & gotchas - Run the wizard rather than entering keys by hand — it validates each key with a real connection to the provider. - One engine works; the ensemble is where the accuracy gain lives. - If dictation misbehaves, check the ensemble health light on the toolbar first — a dead engine usually billing is the most common cause."},{"id":"cycle-window-level","name":"Cycle Window/Level","brief":"One button steps InteleViewer through your window/level presets in order — lung, bone, soft tissue, brain — without reaching for the keyboard.","category":"Measurement & Imaging","tags":"window level presets W/L InteleViewer cycle hotkey","body":"What it does InteleViewer binds window/level presets to function keys, but hitting F4/F5/F7 while your hand is on the mic is awkward. This action sends the next preset key in your configured list to InteleViewer each time you trigger it, cycling through them in order. Put it on a PowerMic button and each press steps you through your presets: soft tissue → lung → bone → back to soft tissue. How to turn it on / set it up 1. Settings → Behavior → InteleViewer Integration → Window/Level cycle keys : enter the comma-separated keys InteleViewer has your presets on, in the order you want to cycle them e.g. F4, F5, F7, F6 . 2. Map the Cycle Window/Level action to a mic button or hotkey under Settings → Keys & Buttons. The keys must match your InteleViewer preset bindings — check InteleViewer's own preferences if you're not sure which function key does what. Using it Press your mapped button; InteleViewer receives the next key in the list and switches presets. Press again for the one after that; the cycle wraps back to the first key at the end. Tips & gotchas - This sends keystrokes to InteleViewer, so it acts on whatever InteleViewer would do with that key — if your presets differ per modality, the same cycle applies everywhere. - Order the list to match your reading habit: the two presets you flip between most should be adjacent so one press toggles between them. - If you'd rather have direct one-press buttons per preset instead of a cycle , the InteleViewer Buttons toolbar does that — each toolbar button can send a single fixed keystroke."},{"id":"dictated-text-highlighting","name":"Dictated Text Highlighting","brief":"Tints the words you dictated in the editor so they stand out from template text, with color tiers flagging the words the transcription ensemble was least sure about.","category":"Report Display & Highlighting","tags":"highlight dictated confidence tint low confidence word coloring STT","body":"What it does When you dictate through Custom STT, the text MosaicTools inserts gets a subtle background tint in the editor, so you can always tell your own words apart from template boilerplate and typed text. On top of that, words are colored in a three-tier confidence system : normal, medium, and low. The tiers are driven by how much the whole transcription ensemble agreed on each word — not just the primary engine's self-reported confidence. A word the primary engine was confident about but the other engines disagreed with gets flagged for a look; pure formatting differences \"cm\" vs \"centimeters\", hyphenation don't flag. How to turn it on / set it up 1. Settings → Experimental → check Use CDP for Mosaic interaction . 2. Check Highlight dictated text underneath it. 3. Requires Custom STT to be your dictation path — this highlights what the ensemble inserted. Using it Dictate normally; skim for the low-confidence colors before you process or sign. That's the whole workflow — the coloring points your eyes at the two or three words per report worth double-checking. The in-editor mode buttons at the bottom-right of the report editor No Highlight / Regular / Rainbow control what's shown: Regular is this confidence tinting, No Highlight hides all dictation highlights Mosaic's native ones too , and Rainbow switches to findings↔impression correlation. Tips & gotchas - Low-confidence coloring stays put in the transcript box after Process Report and survives your edits — the tiers re-map as the text moves. - The tint is intentionally subtle; if you don't see it, check that CDP is connected Settings → Experimental → CDP Status shows a green Mosaic dot . - Your chosen highlight mode persists across restarts and study changes."},{"id":"dictation-indicator","name":"Dictation Indicator & Beeps","brief":"A small floating window shows your words live as you dictate, and start/stop beeps confirm the recorder state without looking away from the images.","category":"Dictation & Speech","tags":"indicator recording live transcription beeps start beep stop beep floating window","body":"What it does Two kinds of feedback keep you confident the recorder heard you. The dictation indicator is a minimal dark floating strip that shows live transcription while you speak — interim words in gray, finalized words in white or confidence-tinted when Text coloring is on, including magenta for a flagged non-word . It clears after each paste, lingers a couple of seconds when recording stops so you can verify the tail of what you said, then hides. The beeps are short tones on recording start and stop, so you know the mic state without ever looking at the screen. How to turn it on / set it up - Indicator: Settings → Speech-to-Text → Display → Show dictation indicator . - Beeps: Settings → Speech-to-Text → Beeps → Start beep / Stop beep , each with its own volume slider. Using it Drag the indicator wherever it doesn't cover anatomy — it remembers its position, grows downward as you speak longer passages, and never steals focus from Mosaic. Most people park it near the report column and stop consciously noticing it, glancing only when a word sounds risky. Tips & gotchas - The indicator shows the driver's live stream; the pasted report text can differ slightly after the ensemble's corrections land — the report is the source of truth. - Keep beeps on even if you drop the indicator: a missing stop beep is the fastest tell that a recording is still running. - There's also a System Beep action you can map to a button for audible dictation-state feedback when using Mosaic's built-in dictation."},{"id":"drafted-findings-popup","name":"Drafted Findings Popup","brief":"A glanceable, prioritized list of a pre-drafted study's findings — acute lines loud and bright, chronic/incidental lines dimmed — with per-term loudness tuning and one-click deletion.","category":"Report Display & Highlighting","tags":"drafted findings popup glanceable loudness tiers acute degenerative prelim AI draft","body":"What it does When a study arrives with an AI preliminary draft, this popup shows you the drafted findings as a prioritized list before you start reading: findings are classified and grouped Acute → Devices & Hardware → Degenerative & Chronic , with \"loud\" findings measurements, acuity terms rendered bold and bright while chronic/incidental boilerplate is dimmed and italicized. Within each section, findings are sorted head-to-toe anatomically, tailored per study type, and the prior-study Comparison line stays pinned at the top. The idea: in one glance you know what the draft thinks matters, before you've scrolled anything. How to turn it on / set it up Settings → Behavior → Show glanceable drafted findings popup . The popup is movable drag from anywhere , auto-resizes to its content, and remembers its position between sessions. Using it - It appears automatically for drafted studies and updates when the draft changes. - Right-click a line to open the loudness tuner: it shows the per-term contributions behind that line's tier, with ▲/▼ arrows to make a term louder or quieter for you permanently. \"Add term from this sentence…\" lets you introduce your own term multi-word phrases work , and \"Sort order\" lets you drag-reorder the body regions per study type. - Click the red X or the finding's text to delete that finding from the report — the deletion is propagated into the Mosaic transcript editor via CDP. - Left-click elsewhere dismisses it for the current case; it re-appears on the next drafted case, and it closes automatically when you sign or discard. Tips & gotchas - Ultrasound \"capture\" studies OCR'd tech worksheets get a flat rendering instead: measurements bold, section labels dimmed, no acuity weighting. - Custom loudness terms match whole words only — a custom \"cyst\" won't fire on \"cystitis\". - Lines beginning with macro/template markers like \"--\" or \" \" are ignored so stray macro text doesn't show up as a finding."},{"id":"ensemble-health","name":"Ensemble Health Light","brief":"A green/yellow/red dot on the main bar shows your speech engines' health at a glance; click it for a per-engine drawer with a plain-language reason when something is wrong.","category":"Dictation & Speech","tags":"health traffic light green yellow red drawer engine down billing quota out of credits","body":"What it does The most common dictation failure isn't a bug — it's an API key quietly running out of credits, leaving the ensemble running a voter short. The health light makes that visible: a small dot at the far left of the MosaicTools toolbar that is green when all engines are healthy, yellow when one is struggling, red when one is down. Click it and a pull-out Ensemble health drawer lists each engine with its own light and, when something is wrong, a normalized plain-language reason you can act on yourself — \"Out of credits — add funds\", \"API key invalid\" — instead of a raw error code. How to turn it on / set it up Nothing to configure. The light appears automatically when you're running the multi-engine ensemble. Using it Glance at the dot between cases; open the drawer only when it isn't green. The drawer refreshes live and dismisses when you click away. When automatic failover is active, the dot becomes a split circle — the left half is your configured ensemble, the right half is what's actually running — so a swap-in is visible at a glance hover for details , and the drawer names the backup covering for the degraded engine \"replaced by …\" . Tips & gotchas - A red light almost always means billing or quota on that engine's account — fixing it is on the provider's website, not in MosaicTools. - You also get a toast notification the moment an engine is detected down, so silent degradation doesn't last a whole shift. - The same live status appears next to each provider in Settings → Speech-to-Text, plus per-slot Test buttons to verify keys proactively."},{"id":"ensemble-metrics","name":"Live Ensemble Metrics","brief":"A draggable always-on-top overlay showing what the ensemble is doing in real time — confidence, per-engine corrections and confirmations, and the measured accuracy impact.","category":"Dictation & Speech","tags":"metrics stats confidence corrections precision accuracy popup overlay","body":"What it does The metrics popup is a window into the ensemble's decision-making. While you dictate it shows the driver's live confidence bar, word counts, and how many low-confidence words were corrected; below that, a section per secondary engine shows its finals, how many driver words it fixed, and how many it confirmed. At the bottom: consensus stats, the last correction it made so you can see exactly what changed and why , and an accuracy impact section — corrections are verified against your final signed reports, so the popup can show real precision figures for the session and all-time, not just activity counts. How to turn it on / set it up Settings → Speech-to-Text → Ensemble Mode → Show live metrics popup . Drag it anywhere; it stays on top and remembers its position. A Clear All-Time Stats button in the same settings area resets the cumulative counters. Using it Most people run without it day to day and turn it on when tuning an ensemble or evaluating a new engine: a secondary that never fixes or confirms anything isn't earning its slot; one with high fix counts and high precision is. Clicking through from the popup opens the full transcript comparison view for a line-by-line look at what each engine heard. Tips & gotchas - Section headers collapse, so you can shrink it to just the confidence bar during normal reading. - All-time stats only accumulate when a report is signed — abandoned dictations don't pollute the precision numbers. - The labels follow your actual configured engines, whatever you've chosen as driver and secondaries."},{"id":"failover-backups","name":"Failover Backup Engines","brief":"Spare engines you've set up but don't run in your ensemble automatically swap in when a member fails mid-shift — dictation keeps its full strength, then hands back when the engine recovers.","category":"Dictation & Speech","tags":"failover backup outage credits degraded spare engine resilience","body":"What it does Cloud engines have outages, and API keys run out of credit — usually mid-shift. Failover turns every engine you've configured but aren't running in your ensemble into a backup pool : when any ensemble member goes down provider outage, exhausted credits, or an engine that stays connected but silently stops producing transcripts , a suitable spare swaps into its slot automatically. Your dictation continues at full ensemble strength instead of quietly running a voter short. When the failed engine recovers, your normal ensemble is restored. How to turn it on / set it up Nothing to enable — any spare configured engine is automatically part of the pool. Settings → Speech-to-Text shows your current pool read-only under the Failover backups divider at the bottom of the ensemble section. The Quick Setup Wizard has an optional Backup engines step that explains this and helps you add a spare for resilience. Using it You'll usually learn a failover happened from the health light: the toolbar dot becomes a split circle left half = configured ensemble, right half = what's actually running , and the health drawer names the substitution — \"replaced by …\". Fix the underlying account when convenient; nothing is urgent while the backup is covering. Tips & gotchas - Failovers hold steady deliberately — hysteresis prevents flip-flopping every time an engine's health briefly wavers. - A backup can only cover a role it's capable of a batch-only engine can't stand in as the streaming driver . - The best cheap insurance is one extra key you never otherwise use — pay-per-use engines cost nothing while idle."},{"id":"findings-impression-checker","name":"Findings/Impression Mismatch","brief":"Catches outright contradictions between FINDINGS and IMPRESSION — \"no bowel obstruction\" in the findings with \"bowel obstruction\" in the impression — and pulses an alert until you fix or dismiss it.","category":"Report Processing & QA","tags":"contradiction findings impression mismatch negation qa alerts fim","body":"What it does After both sections exist, MosaicTools compares FINDINGS and IMPRESSION for contradictions of assertion: one section negates a concept the other asserts. The classic case is a normal-template findings line \"No evidence of bowel obstruction\" surviving next to a dictated impression point \"Small bowel obstruction\" — usually the residue of an edited template or a processing hiccup. When it finds one, the notification box pulses the settings label marks it pulsing orange and names the contradiction. How to turn it on / set it up Settings → Alerts → Alert Triggers → Findings/Impression mismatch. It requires the notification box Enable Notification Box , same tab and appears even in alerts-only mode. Using it The check runs automatically as the report is scraped — in practice you notice it right after Process Report. It matches medical concepts through synonym patterns plus proximity matching all the significant words of a term in the same sentence , so \"SBO\" phrasings and reworded equivalents are still caught. With CDP active, the contradicting text flashes in orange in the report editor itself until corrected. Click the alert to dismiss; it clears on study change. Tips & gotchas - Direction matters and is reported: findings-negates/impression-asserts is the common one, but the reverse fires too. - It's a concept-level check, not a grammar check — it won't catch a contradiction phrased in terms it has no concept for. Treat it as a net, not a guarantee. - Number and side discrepancies are the job of the Measurement & laterality consistency checker; run both."},{"id":"fix-transcript","name":"Fix Transcript","brief":"A mappable action that presses Mosaic's transcript-cleanup button (the wrench icon) for you — no mousing to the Transcript toolbar.","category":"Dictation & Speech","tags":"fix transcript wrench cleanup transcript action hotkey","body":"What it does Mosaic's Transcript editor has a cleanup button — the wrench-in-a-circle icon — that tidies the dictated transcript. Fix Transcript is a MosaicTools action that clicks it for you, so you can trigger the cleanup from a PowerMic/SpeechMike button or a keyboard hotkey without leaving the images or hunting for the icon. How to turn it on / set it up Nothing to enable — just map it. Open Settings → Keys & Buttons and bind Fix Transcript to a spare mic button or hotkey, like any other action. Using it After dictating a chunk, hit the mapped button; MosaicTools finds the wrench in the Transcript editor toolbar and presses it. The transcript cleans up exactly as if you had clicked it yourself — this action adds convenience, not a different cleanup. Tips & gotchas - The action drives Mosaic's own cleanup — what it fixes and doesn't is Mosaic's behavior, identical to a manual click on the wrench. - It works through MosaicTools' direct Mosaic access CDP ; if the button can't be found — say, no transcript is open — the action logs it and does nothing rather than clicking blindly. - It pairs well with a dictation-heavy workflow: dictate, Fix Transcript, then Process Report, all from mic buttons without touching the mouse."},{"id":"gender-check","name":"Gender Check","brief":"Flashing red alert when the report contains gender-specific terms that don't fit the patient — a prostate in a female patient's report, a uterus in a male's.","category":"Report Processing & QA","tags":"gender sex wrong patient prostate uterus mismatch alerts","body":"What it does A gender-specific organ in the wrong patient's report almost always means one of two things: a template with leftover organ lines, or dictation landing in the wrong chart. Either way you want to know before signing. Gender check watches the drafted report for gender-specific terms that contradict the patient's sex and raises a flashing red border on the notification box when it finds one. How to turn it on / set it up Settings → Alerts → Alert Triggers → Gender check. Like the other alert triggers it needs the notification box enabled Enable Notification Box , same tab and fires even in alerts-only mode. Using it Nothing to do during a normal case — it's silent until it trips. When it does, the notification box flashes red, and with the CDP connection active the offending text also flashes red inside the report editor itself until you correct it, so the exact word is easy to find in a long report. Tips & gotchas - The most common trigger is benign: a stock template mentioning the uterus/prostate in its normal lines on a mismatched patient. It's still worth the two seconds to check — the rare true wrong-patient case looks identical. - It reads the patient's sex from the scraped study data, so it needs background Mosaic scraping running it is by default . - Pair it with the Patient mismatch alert: gender check catches wrong-sex content inside the report, patient mismatch catches Mosaic and InteleViewer showing two different patients."},{"id":"get-prior","name":"Get Prior","brief":"Grabs the relevant prior study's date, time, and description from InteleViewer, formats it as your COMPARISON line, and pastes it into Mosaic — one button, no typing.","category":"Priors & Clinical History","tags":"prior comparison paste InteleViewer prior study comparison line","body":"What it does Instead of reading the prior's date off InteleViewer and dictating \"Comparison: CT chest from …\", Get Prior extracts the prior study's information from InteleViewer — date, time, and study description — formats it through your comparison template, and pastes the finished line into the Mosaic report. It parses the raw InteleViewer text including the timezone-stamped time into a clean date, so the pasted line always reads the same way regardless of how InteleViewer displayed it. How to turn it on / set it up 1. Map the Get Prior action to a mic button or hotkey under Settings → Keys & Buttons. 2. Customize the output under Settings → Text Templates → Get Prior Comparison , using placeholders: {date} , {time} , {description} , and {noimages} the text to use when there's no prior . Using it 1. Have the prior selected/visible in InteleViewer. 2. Trigger Get Prior. 3. The formatted COMPARISON text lands in your Mosaic report. Done — carry on dictating. The same prior information also feeds the Comparison line pinned at the top of the drafted-findings popup, so drafted cases show what the draft was compared against. Tips & gotchas - The extraction is text-based: if InteleViewer's prior text is oddly formatted or nothing prior-like is on screen, nothing pastes — check that the prior is actually the active/selected study in InteleViewer and try again. - Get Prior gives you the reference line ; if you want to know what the prior actually said , use Summarize Prior — it opens the full prior report with an AI summary on top. The two actions complement each other and many people map both. - The {time} placeholder is there for sites/modalities where same-day priors matter e.g. ICU chest films ; most people's templates use just {date} and {description} ."},{"id":"headless-mode","name":"Headless Mode","brief":"Launch with -headless and MosaicTools runs invisibly from the system tray — no widget bar, no hotkeys, but your mic buttons and toasts still work.","category":"Setup & Platform","tags":"headless tray minimal no bar command line","body":"What it does Headless mode strips MosaicTools down to the invisible essentials for people who want the automation without any on-screen chrome. There's no widget bar and no global keyboard hotkeys — just a system-tray icon. PowerMic/SpeechMike buttons, the InteleViewer floating toolbar, and toast notifications all still work, so every mic-triggered action behaves exactly as in normal mode. How to turn it on / set it up Start the exe with the flag: MosaicTools.exe -headless To make it permanent, add -headless to whatever launches MosaicTools a shortcut's Target field, or your startup entry . The tray icon reads \"Mosaic Tools Headless \" so you can tell which mode you're in. Using it Everything runs from the tray icon: - Double-click it to open Settings. - Right-click for Settings , Show Log , and Exit . Trigger actions with your mic buttons as usual. Toasts still appear at the bottom-right for feedback action confirmations, warnings, update notices . Tips & gotchas - Keyboard hotkeys are intentionally disabled in this mode — if you rely on hotkeys rather than mic buttons, headless isn't for you. - Bar-dependent displays TBWU metrics, critical count badge, ensemble health dot, connectivity dots have nowhere to render; the underlying services still run, you just don't see the indicators. - Settings are shared between modes — configure everything in normal mode first, then switch to headless once you're happy. - Desktop-only sections like the recording indicator options hide themselves in the Settings window when running headless."},{"id":"impression-fixer","name":"Impression Fixer","brief":"One-click buttons that insert or replace impression text — your standard closers and disclaimers applied from the report popup (or directly inside the Mosaic editor) without dictating them.","category":"Worklist & Productivity","tags":"impression quick insert replace fixer boilerplate report popup buttons","body":"What it does Shows quick-action buttons next to the IMPRESSION line after you process a report. Each button is one of your configured entries and either inserts its text into the impression or replaces the impression with it — for the boilerplate you'd otherwise dictate or type on half your studies \"No acute intracranial abnormality.\", comparison-limited disclaimers, follow-up lines . How to turn it on / set it up 1. Check Enable Impression Fixer in Settings → Text & Templates → Impression Fixer . 2. Click Edit... to manage entries. Each entry has: - A blurb the button label and the text to apply. - Insert vs Replace mode. - Match criteria required / any-of / exclude terms against the study description so buttons only appear where they make sense. - An optional comparison requirement with a max-age-in-weeks — e.g. only offer a \"stable compared to prior\" entry when a recent comparison actually exists. 3. Optional: check Impression fixer buttons in editor Settings → Experimental, under Mosaic CDP to also render the buttons directly in the Mosaic report editor next to IMPRESSION. Requires CDP to be enabled. Using it After Process Report, the matching buttons appear on the report popup's IMPRESSION line and in the editor itself if the CDP option is on . Click one; the text is applied to the impression immediately. Tips & gotchas - Replace mode overwrites the whole impression — best kept for normal-study closers where that's exactly what you want. - Criteria keep the button row short; an entry with no criteria shows on every study. - The in-editor buttons need Use CDP for Mosaic interaction enabled; the report-popup buttons work without CDP."},{"id":"impression-popup","name":"Impression Popup","brief":"A small floating window that shows just the IMPRESSION after Process Report, so you can verify the conclusion without scrolling the report.","category":"Report Display & Highlighting","tags":"impression popup floating window process report transparent","body":"What it does After Process Report, this floating window displays the IMPRESSION section by itself — the part you actually need to sanity-check before signing. It appears automatically once the impression is generated and hides itself when you sign the report, so it tracks your normal rhythm: process, glance at the impression, sign. How to turn it on / set it up Settings → Report Display → check Show Impression popup after Process Report . That's it — no action mapping needed. Like the report popup, it supports two rendering styles: a solid dark window, or a transparent layered mode where the image shows through behind the text. It shares the Report Display transparency settings. Using it - The popup appears near your images after each Process Report and updates for each new study. - Click it to dismiss it for the current case; it auto-hides when you sign. - Drag it wherever you like — it's a floating always-on-top window. Tips & gotchas - If you want the whole report not just the impression with change/correlation highlighting, use the Report Popup instead — the two can run together, but most people pick one. - The popup is a display of the impression; to edit it, either dictate/edit in Mosaic as usual, or use the report popup's delete buttons on impression points Settings → Report Display → \"Show delete buttons on impression points\" . - Pairs well with the Findings/Impression mismatch alert Settings → Alerts — the popup gives you the impression to read, the alert flags a contradiction you might have missed."},{"id":"independent-scrolling","name":"Independent Column Scrolling","brief":"Makes Mosaic's Transcript, Report, and utility columns scroll independently instead of the whole page moving at once.","category":"Report Display & Highlighting","tags":"scrolling columns transcript report utility scroll fix CDP","body":"What it does By default, Mosaic scrolls its layout as one page: scroll the report and the transcript goes with it. With this on, MosaicTools injects styling via CDP so the Transcript, Report, and sidebar/utility columns each scroll on their own . You can park the transcript where you want it, scroll the Final Report to the impression, and keep a utility panel pinned — each column holds its own position. How to turn it on / set it up 1. Settings → Experimental → check Use CDP for Mosaic interaction Mosaic restart needed on first enable . 2. Check Independent column scrolling underneath it. Using it Once enabled, it's transparent — the columns just behave the way you'd expect a modern app to behave. A defensive scroll guard also runs inside the report editor to catch Mosaic's habit of yanking the report back to the top a second after an edit, reverting those unwanted jumps within about 150 ms. Tips & gotchas - Recording border : as of 5.5.3, Mosaic's native red \"recording\" outline around the panel is no longer clipped to tiny corner dots while this is on — the full red border shows during recording, matching the scrolling-off behavior. - Opening Mosaic's Settings/Profile page used to leave the report unscrollable with a clipped toolbar; the styling now cleans itself up when you leave the report view and re-applies when you return. - If you use the RadPair page inside Mosaic and see layout weirdness there, turning this off is the known workaround — the injected CSS can interact badly with that page. - Like all Mosaic CDP features, a Mosaic update can temporarily change behavior until MosaicTools adjusts."},{"id":"installation-updates","name":"Installation & Updates","brief":"A single exe with no installer and no admin rights, which quietly keeps itself up to date — updates stage in the background and apply only when it's safe, with a What's New popup and one-click rollback.","category":"Setup & Platform","tags":"install update auto-update whats new rollback version github zip","body":"What it does MosaicTools is one MosaicTools.exe — no installer, no admin rights, no services. Drop it in a folder and run it. It updates itself from GitHub Releases: new versions download as a ZIP which corporate antivirus tolerates far better than a bare exe download and install via a rename trick — the running exe is renamed aside, the new one takes its place, and the app restarts into it. How to turn it on / set it up Settings → Desktop Mode → Updates : - Auto-update on startup — check for and install updates when the app launches. - Check Now — manual check. - Rollback to Previous Version — pick any earlier release and install it; auto-update is disabled afterwards so it doesn't immediately re-update you. Using it Mostly nothing: long-running sessions also check hourly in the background, stage the update silently, and apply it at a dictation lull — never mid-dictation. After an update you get the What's New popup summarizing everything since the version you last saw skip three updates, see all three sets of notes . The full history is always available via the 🆕 What's New button at the top of Settings. Tips & gotchas - Updates come from the public MosaicTools-dist GitHub repo; only outbound HTTPS to github.com is needed. The updater honors your system proxy and authenticates with your Windows credentials, which matters behind authenticating corporate proxies. - An anti-loop guard backs off for a cooldown if an update can't stick locked exe, AV quarantine rather than restart-looping. - Don't run MosaicTools from a folder you can't write to, or from OneDrive-synced paths if you can avoid it — the rename swap needs write access next to the exe. - Rollback is the escape hatch if a new version misbehaves; report the issue so it's fixed, then re-enable Auto-update on startup ."},{"id":"iv-toolbar","name":"InteleViewer Buttons Toolbar","brief":"A floating, always-on-top toolbar of custom buttons that send your favorite InteleViewer shortcuts — designed in the built-in Button Studio.","category":"Measurement & Imaging","tags":"toolbar floating buttons InteleViewer button studio shortcuts presets","body":"What it does A small frameless toolbar that floats on top of your viewer and holds configurable buttons for InteleViewer shortcuts — window/level presets, zoom, or any keystroke you'd otherwise reach for. Click a button, the keystroke goes to InteleViewer. It stays on top, drags anywhere by its top bar, and remembers its position. How to turn it on / set it up 1. Settings → Keys & Buttons → InteleViewer Buttons → check Show InteleViewer Buttons toolbar . 2. Click Open Button Studio... to design it. In Button Studio you build the button set: - Add square icon or wide labeled buttons. - Give each an icon from the built-in library and/or a text label. - Assign what it does: a keystroke to send to InteleViewer, or a MosaicTools action. - Set the number of columns to control the toolbar's shape tall strip vs. compact grid . - A live preview shows the toolbar as you edit; changes apply when saved. Using it Park the toolbar at the edge of a monitor near your images. Click buttons instead of hunting for function keys — useful when your hands are on the mouse and mic rather than the keyboard. Dragging by the top bar repositions it; the position persists across sessions. Tips & gotchas - Buttons that send keystrokes act on InteleViewer — make sure the key you assign actually matches your InteleViewer binding for that function. - For things you do constantly, a physical mic button Keys & Buttons is still faster; the toolbar earns its keep for the second tier of shortcuts you use a few times an hour. - The toolbar is hidden in headless mode contexts where there's no InteleViewer workflow."},{"id":"keys-buttons","name":"Keys & Buttons","brief":"Map any MosaicTools action to a PowerMic button, a SpeechMike button, or a global keyboard hotkey — every action, one dialog.","category":"Setup & Platform","tags":"powermic speechmike hotkeys key mappings buttons action mapping microphone","body":"What it does Every MosaicTools action — Get Prior, Critical Findings, Process Report, Sign Report, Show Pick Lists, MTRec Recommendations, Auto Measure, and the rest — can be triggered from your dictation mic's buttons or a global hotkey. The Key Mappings dialog shows one row per action with a hotkey box and a mic-button dropdown. How to turn it on / set it up 1. Settings → Keys & Buttons . Under Dictation Microphone , pick Auto , PowerMic , or SpeechMike Auto connects to whatever is plugged in ; the status line shows what's detected. 2. Click Open Keys Configuration... In the dialog, choose the device to configure PowerMic or SpeechMike — each keeps its own mapping set , then per action: - Click the hotkey box and press a key combination to capture it. - Or pick a mic button from the dropdown. PowerMic buttons: Left Button, Right Button, Record Button, T Button, Skip Back, Skip Forward, Rewind, Fast Forward, Stop/Play, Checkmark, Scan Button. SpeechMike buttons: Record, Play/Pause, Fast Forward, Rewind, EoL, Ins/Ovr, -i-, Index Finger, F1–F4. Using it Press the button; the action runs. MosaicTools reads the mic's HID interface directly, so mappings work regardless of which window has focus, and focus is saved/restored around Mosaic interactions. Tips & gotchas - The settings page also lists the Hardcoded Mic Buttons — a default set e.g. PowerMic Skip Back → Process Report, Record Button → Start/Stop Dictation, Checkmark → Sign Report; SpeechMike Ins/Ovr, -i-, Record, EoL for the same four so a fresh install works before you customize anything. - SpeechMike works in Event mode; use Philips SpeechControl if you want the front mouse-style buttons behaving as hotkeys. - Global hotkeys are disabled in headless mode — mic buttons still work there. - MosaicTools tags its own injected keystrokes so an Alt-key mapping can't re-trigger itself."},{"id":"keyterms","name":"Keyterms & Auto-Learning","brief":"Radiology vocabulary sent to the speech engines to bias recognition toward the right words — plus auto-learning that harvests new terms from your own signed reports.","category":"Dictation & Speech","tags":"keyterms vocabulary boosting auto-learn phrase list radiology terms custom words","body":"What it does Speech engines let you supply a list of \"keyterms\" — words to favor when the audio is ambiguous, which is exactly where radiology vocabulary lives periportal, echogenicity, spondylolisthesis . MosaicTools maintains that list for you in three tiers: your manual terms, auto-learned terms, and a built-in radiology backfill database that fills whatever slots remain. The merged list is sent to every engine in your ensemble, formatted however each one needs it Whisper gets it as a prompt string . Auto-learning watches for low-confidence words during dictation and verifies them against the final signed report — if you kept the word, it's a real term the engine struggled with, and it earns a keyterm slot. Each engine keeps its own learned list, since each mishears different things. How to turn it on / set it up - Manual terms: Settings → Speech-to-Text → Keyterms box, one per line a Sort A–Z button and a live count are next to it . - Auto-learning: check Auto-learn keyterms from reports . Per-engine stats rows Deepgram, Soniox, Speechmatics, AssemblyAI, ElevenLabs, OpenAI… show how many terms each has learned, with a View button to inspect or prune them. Using it It's invisible in daily use — recognition of your recurring hard words simply improves over weeks as the learned lists grow. Tips & gotchas - Every engine caps its keyterm count, so priority matters: manual terms win, and an auto-learned term misrecognized at least 3 times jumps ahead of the rest. Duplicates across tiers are never sent twice. - Keyterms bias recognition; they don't force it. For deterministic swaps use Custom word replacements under Text Processing instead."},{"id":"macros","name":"Macros","brief":"Auto-inserts your text snippets into the transcript when a study matching your criteria opens — with per-macro study-description rules, a drafted-only option, and optional auto-processing afterwards.","category":"Worklist & Productivity","tags":"macro template auto-insert study description text insertion drafted only","body":"What it does When a study opens whose description matches a macro's criteria, MosaicTools types that macro's text into the transcript automatically — standard comments, technique lines, or a full report skeleton, placed before you say a word. How to turn it on / set it up 1. Check Enable Macros in Settings → Text & Templates → Macros requires Scrape Mosaic to be on . 2. Click Edit... to open the Macro Editor. Each macro has: - Criteria — required terms, any-of terms, and exclude terms matched against the study description. - Drafted studies only — only fire when Mosaic marked the study as having an AI preliminary draft. - A voice option, plus per-macro enable/disable. 3. Optional: Add blank lines before macro text inserts blank lines above the macro so you have dictation space. 4. Optional: Auto-process all studies after macro insertion Settings → Behavior waits for all opening macros to land, then runs Process Report automatically. Using it Open a matching study and the text is just there. Multiple macros can fire on one study if several match. Tips & gotchas - Drafted-study timing is deliberate : Mosaic silently reverts a macro pasted too early onto a drafted study, so MosaicTools waits default 3000 ms after the draft renders. The Drafted-macro paste delay and Drafted-state wait controls in Settings → Behavior tune this — read the in-app warnings before lowering them. - If you use drafted-only macros, non -drafted studies pay the full Drafted-state wait default 5000 ms at open while MosaicTools waits to see whether the drafted flag appears. - Macros can be referenced from pick-list nodes by ID; the editor preserves those references across edits. - MosaicTools' own macro insertions don't count as \"unprocessed dictation\" for the premature-sign warning."},{"id":"mic-gain-calibrator","name":"Mic Gain Calibrator","brief":"A built-in tool that listens to you dictate and recommends the right Windows microphone gain — so the speech engines get clean audio, neither too quiet nor clipping.","category":"Dictation & Speech","tags":"microphone gain audio setup levels calibration too quiet clipping","body":"What it does Recognition accuracy starts at the microphone. A gain set too low buries your voice in the noise floor; too high and loud syllables clip and garble. The Mic Gain Calibrator opens a window that captures your microphone live, shows your speech levels as you dictate a few sentences, and computes a Recommended Windows Mic Gain percentage for your specific mic and voice. You then set that value in Windows sound settings or confirm it already matches . How to turn it on / set it up Settings → Profile → Mic Gain Calibrator . It grants itself microphone access and starts listening; no configuration needed. It runs on the WebView2 runtime, which is present on any machine running Mosaic. Using it Open it once when you first set up custom dictation, speak the way you actually dictate — normal reading-room volume, normal distance from the mic — and read off the recommended gain. Re-run it whenever your audio situation changes: a new microphone, a new headset, a new workstation, or if colleagues' transcripts suggest your levels drifted. Tips & gotchas - Calibrate with the microphone you actually dictate with, positioned as you actually use it — calibrating a desk mic from six inches and then dictating from two feet defeats the point. - The free on-device medASR engine is the most sensitive to audio quality of all the engines; a correct gain and a wired mic rather than Bluetooth is what keeps it a strong voter. - If the window comes up blank, the WebView2 runtime is missing — the dialog links to Microsoft's download page."},{"id":"mosaic-zoom","name":"Mosaic Zoom Memory","brief":"Mosaic forgets your Ctrl+scroll text-size zoom on every launch; MosaicTools remembers it and re-applies it silently.","category":"Setup & Platform","tags":"zoom text size ctrl scroll page zoom mosaic restore","body":"What it does Mosaic loses your text-size zoom the Ctrl+scroll / Ctrl +/- page zoom every time it launches, so anyone who reads at anything other than 100% has been re-zooming daily. With this on, MosaicTools remembers the zoom level you set and re-applies it automatically the next time you're working in Mosaic. How to turn it on / set it up 1. Enable CDP if you haven't: Settings → Experimental → Mosaic CDP → Use CDP for Mosaic interaction this feature requires it . 2. Check Remember & restore my Mosaic zoom level in Settings → Behavior → Mosaic Zoom . 3. Set your preferred zoom once in Mosaic with Ctrl+scroll or Ctrl +/-. Done. Using it Nothing to do day-to-day. When Mosaic launches fresh at the default zoom, MosaicTools applies your remembered level the moment Mosaic is focused. If you change the zoom during a shift, the new level becomes the remembered one. Tips & gotchas - The restore is deliberately unobtrusive: it applies silently while Mosaic is focused and never moves, resizes, or steals focus from your windows. - This is the text-size page zoom inside Mosaic's report area, not your PACS/InteleViewer image zoom. - If restore stops working, check the CDP status dashboard Settings → Experimental → CDP Status — the Mosaic dot should be green. CDP requires a Mosaic restart the first time it's enabled."},{"id":"mtrec-associations","name":"MTRec Associations","brief":"A completeness checklist against satisfaction-of-search — for each major finding you dictate, a small popup lists the companion findings that usually travel with it, marked addressed, missing, or N/A for this study type. It never inserts text.","category":"Recommendations","tags":"associations completeness checklist companion findings satisfaction of search mode b","body":"What it does Satisfaction of search is the enemy: you call the pulmonary embolism and move on without commenting on right-heart strain. Associations is a second MTRec pass built against exactly that. When a primary finding is detected — cirrhosis, bowel obstruction, PE, hydronephrosis, pancreatic mass, midline shift, intracranial hemorrhage, AAA, and ~27 categories in all across five groups Severity & physiology, Complications, Tumor staging, Extent & source, acute OB-Gyn — a small popup lists its companion findings, each marked: - ✓ green — the report addresses it, in either polarity: a negated companion \"no portal vein thrombosis\" counts as addressed , because you looked. - ● red — not addressed anywhere in the report. - – N/A grey — not assessable on this study type, with the reason portal-vein clot on a non-contrast CT, marrow edema on a CT rather than MR . It is read-only by design. It never inserts or changes text , and the recommendation pass is unaffected. How to turn it on / set it up Settings → MTRec → Enable MTRec Associations off by default . Under it: - Show extended companions secondary tier — adds the dimmer, complementary items a thorough read covers anyway clot burden for PE, underlying cause for an obstruction . Core lists are always shown. - Per-group and per-bucket checkboxes control exactly which associations fire — toggle a whole type or a single finding. Using it The checklist appears on the MTRec key press again to refresh , or via the amber boxes after Process Report. Glance, cover the red dots in your dictation, click anywhere to dismiss. It remembers its position, and closes itself when you sign or discard the study. Tips & gotchas Companion detection was corpus-validated across thousands of real reports 5.4.17 , so spurious checklists are rare — but a red dot is a prompt to consider , not an assertion that something is wrong. The \"Insert the missing negative statement\" option in Settings is a placeholder and currently does nothing."},{"id":"mtrec-boxes","name":"MTRec Boxes","brief":"After Process Report, small clickable boxes appear next to \"Final Report\" — green \"+\" boxes insert a guideline recommendation into the impression with one click, amber \"⚠\" boxes are association reminders.","category":"Recommendations","tags":"mtrec boxes chips process report one-click insert green plus amber final report","body":"What it does Instead of pressing the MTRec key and having recommendations merge immediately, the boxes give you a look-then-click flow. After you Process Report, once the impression has generated, MTRec runs automatically and posts its results as small boxes next to \"Final Report\" e.g. \"+ renal\" : - Green boxes are available recommendations. Click one and it's inserted into the impression; nothing is inserted until you click , and Ctrl+Z undoes an insert. - Amber boxes are association reminders only if MTRec Associations is enabled . Hover to see which companion findings the report hasn't addressed; click to open the full checklist modal. How to turn it on / set it up Settings → MTRec → \"Show MTRec boxes after Process Report\" — on by default once the MTRec engine itself is enabled. The manual MTRec Recommendations key keeps working exactly as before and is unaffected by this toggle. Using it Process the report, glance right of \"Final Report\". No boxes = MTRec found nothing actionable. A green \"+ thyroid\" means a guideline follow-up is one click away; click it, check the wording in the impression, move on. An amber \"⚠ cirrhosis\" is a nudge, not text — hover for the missing companions or click for the checklist. Debug information optional Settings → MTRec → \"Include debug information on the boxes\" available when the boxes are on adds a look-under-the-hood layer: - Hover a box to see the recommendation, a divider, and the exact dictated FINDINGS text the recommendation was based on — so you can tell at a glance why MTRec fired. - Right-click any box to copy the full, de-identified MTRec debug for the whole study to the clipboard a small \"debug copied\" note pops up . Left-click still inserts as normal. The copied debug includes the entire dictation every section, de-identified , not just the parsed FINDINGS — so a recommendation MTRec missed can be spotted from the full text. - \"no MTRec actionable items\" — when debug is on and MTRec ran but found no recommendation or a"},{"id":"mtrec","name":"MTRec Recommendations","brief":"The in-house, deterministic recommendation engine — detects incidental findings in your report and merges the matching guideline follow-up recommendation, with citation, into the impression on a single key.","category":"Recommendations","tags":"mtrec recommendations fleischner bosniak ti-rads o-rads incidental findings follow-up guidelines citations","body":"What it does MTRec reads your FINDINGS and IMPRESSION and, on one keypress, inserts guideline follow-up recommendations for the incidental findings it detects — pulmonary nodules, thyroid, adrenal, renal, ovarian, liver, gallbladder, spleen, lymph nodes, biliary, pancreatic, AAA/thoracic aorta, plus trauma grading. Every recommendation ends with its source: Fleischner 2017, Bosniak v2019, ACR TI-RADS, O-RADS, the ACR Incidental Findings white papers, SRU gallbladder consensus, SVS aorta guidelines, AAST organ injury scaling 2025 kidney update , and more. The safety contract is the point: no AI ever authors a clinical number . Every threshold comes from verified guideline tables, the engine runs entirely offline inside MosaicTools, and its behavior is pinned by 480+ self-tests. It replaced the external RecoMD service — no agent, no window-scraping, instant. How to turn it on / set it up Settings → MTRec → Enable MTRec recommendation engine , then assign MTRec Recommendations to a key or mic button under Keys & Buttons . Optional settings on the same tab: - Extraction engine — locked to \"Deterministic offline, verified tables \". An \"AI-assisted\" mode is present in the UI but disabled for now not ready for release ; the deterministic engine is always what runs. - Relocate recommendations that ended up in FINDINGS into the impression R6 — off by default. Using it Press the key after your findings are in; recommendations merge into the impression, placed last. It's smart about noise: it reports the largest lesion with multiplicity/laterality, respects stability \"unchanged for 3 years\" clears the guideline window instead of restarting the clock , reads the clinical history no incidental-nodule follow-up in a known-cancer study , and replaces a matching point already in your impression rather than duplicating it. Ctrl+Z undoes the whole insert. Thyroid, ovarian, and renal cysts and nodules are always carried into the impression since 5.5.4 , independent of the relocate-rec"},{"id":"patient-mismatch","name":"Patient Mismatch Alert","brief":"Pulsing yellow alert when InteleViewer is showing a different patient than the one open in Mosaic — the classic setup for dictating one patient's images into another's report.","category":"Report Processing & QA","tags":"wrong patient inteleviewer mosaic mismatch safety alerts","body":"What it does The highest-stakes desynchronization on a PACS workstation is images and report belonging to two different people. MosaicTools continuously compares the patient name in Mosaic against the topmost InteleViewer window, and when they disagree the notification box gets a pulsing yellow border. It's the first-listed alert trigger for a reason. How to turn it on / set it up Settings → Alerts → Alert Triggers → Patient mismatch. Requires the notification box to be enabled Enable Notification Box on the same tab ; the alert shows even in alerts-only mode. Using it It runs in the background off the normal scrape loop — you only see it when Mosaic and InteleViewer diverge. That legitimately happens for a moment whenever you advance one application before the other, so the useful signal is a pulse that persists : if the yellow border is still going once both apps have settled, stop and reconcile which patient you're actually reading before dictating another word. Tips & gotchas - Brief pulses during study transitions are normal and expected; they clear on their own as the two applications catch up. - Name comparison is deliberately tolerant of formatting differences between the two systems, and false alerts were further tightened in 5.4.5 — a persistent alert deserves attention. - It compares against the topmost InteleViewer window; with multiple InteleViewer windows open, make sure the one on top is the one you're reading from. - This alert is about the viewer/reporter pair. Wrong-sex content already inside the report is the Gender check's job."},{"id":"pick-lists","name":"Pick Lists","brief":"Structured text insertion from a popup — navigate a hierarchical tree or build sentences category-by-category, and paste the result straight into the report.","category":"Worklist & Productivity","tags":"pick list structured insertion tree builder sentence builder templated findings","body":"What it does Presents a popup of your configured pick lists for the current study and pastes what you select. Two modes per list: - Tree — hierarchical navigation with breadcrumbs; drill into categories and pick items structured or freeform accumulation . - Builder — sentence construction: step through categories in order location → size → character… , and the popup assembles the sentence as you go. Tree nodes can embed Builder lists and vice versa, so a tree item can drop you into a sentence builder mid-flow. How to turn it on / set it up 1. Check Enable Pick Lists in Settings → Text & Templates → Pick Lists . 2. Click Edit... to create lists in the Pick List Editor — define each list's trigger criteria study description , mode, and items. Items can also reference macros. 3. Map the Show Pick Lists action to a button or hotkey in Settings → Keys & Buttons the settings page reminds you: Assign \"Show Pick Lists\" action in Keys dialog . 4. Options: - Skip list when only one matches — jumps straight into the list instead of showing a one-item chooser. - Keep window open — the popup stays up after inserting, so you can fire several items with the number keys. Using it Press the mapped button, pick with mouse, arrow keys, number keys, or the search box; the text pastes into the report at the cursor. In Builder mode, finish the sentence and it pastes as a unit; undo steps back one selection. Tips & gotchas - Pick lists match on the study description, so a study matching zero lists shows nothing — check your trigger criteria if the popup seems empty. - Number keys are the fast path with Keep window open : press the digit, item inserts, popup stays. - Editing macros referenced by pick lists is safe — references are preserved by ID."},{"id":"process-report","name":"Process Report","brief":"The one-button core of the workflow — sends Alt+P to Mosaic to structure your dictation into the report, wrapped with auto-stop dictation, smart scrolling, and every post-process surface MosaicTools offers.","category":"Report Processing & QA","tags":"process alt+p dictation structure report auto-stop smart scroll line count hide history","body":"What it does Process Report is the center of the MosaicTools workflow. The action itself sends Alt+P to Mosaic, which runs Mosaic's own LLM to structure your dictation into the report. What MosaicTools adds is everything around that keypress: it can stop your dictation first, scroll the report so the IMPRESSION is visible, and light up all the post-process surfaces — the report popup, the impression popup, MTRec boxes, and if enabled RecoMD/RadAI. How to turn it on / set it up Map Process Report to a mic button or hotkey under Settings → Keys & Buttons . The wrappers each have their own toggle: - Settings → Desktop → Auto-Stop Dictation on Process Report — stops dictation when you press it with an optional Auto-Restart Dictation after Process Report sub-option so you can keep dictating into the impression . - Settings → Behavior → Scroll to bottom on Process Report — scrolls so the impression is on screen, with Smart Scroll Thresholds lines controlling how many PgDn presses fire for longer reports, and Show line count toast for a quick length readout. - Settings → Behavior → Hide clinical history during Process Report — transiently hides the clinical history while Mosaic's LLM runs, so it can't hallucinate a finding that appears only in the history. Nondestructive; the history is restored immediately after processing. Using it Dictate, press the button once, and watch the surfaces appear: the structured report lands in Mosaic, the report/impression popups open if enabled, and once the impression generates, MTRec runs and shows its clickable boxes next to \"Final Report\". Tips & gotchas If you triggered it while dictating with auto-stop on, MosaicTools waits for dictation to fully stop and finalize before processing — one press is enough. Process Report is also what arms the sign-warning: dictating after processing and then signing without re-processing triggers the warning box."},{"id":"punctuation-formatting","name":"Punctuation & Formatting","brief":"Controls how dictation becomes report text — auto vs. spoken punctuation, new-line behavior, radiology notation cleanup, and your own word replacements.","category":"Dictation & Speech","tags":"punctuation auto-punctuate period comma new line contractions radiology cleanup dates units replacements","body":"What it does Between the speech engines and your report sits a text-processing layer you control: - Auto-punctuate — on, the engines insert punctuation for you; off, you dictate it \"period\", \"comma\" . With it off, punctuation you actually speak is kept while model-guessed punctuation is dropped, so the transcript reflects what you said. - Auto-punctuate in final report only — a middle path: dictate punctuation in the transcript, but text dictated directly into the final-report editor still gets auto-punctuation. - New line after every sentence — a line break after each sentence-ending period, for list-style findings. - Radiology cleanup — converts spoken spine levels, units, dimensions, and dates to standard notation say \"el four five\" and get L4-5; \"three point two by two point one centimeters\" formats as numerals . - Expand contractions — \"there's\" becomes \"there is\"; reports read formally. - Custom word replacements — your own find→replace table for words the engines persistently get wrong or phrasing you always normalize; case is preserved automatically, and each row can be toggled. How to turn it on / set it up Everything lives in Settings → Speech-to-Text, under the Punctuation and Text Processing dividers. The replacements grid has +/− buttons to add and remove rows. Tips & gotchas - Auto-punctuate and \"final report only\" are mutually exclusive — checking the first disables the second. - Replacements are word-for-word and deterministic; for biasing recognition toward a hard term, use keyterms instead. - These rules run identically whether Turbo Paste is on or off — they're applied to whatever the ensemble produces."},{"id":"push-to-talk","name":"Push-to-Talk (Dead Man's Switch)","brief":"Hold the Record button to dictate, release to stop — recording can never be left running by accident.","category":"Dictation & Speech","tags":"push to talk ptt dead man's switch hold record powermic speechmike Dead Man's Switch","body":"What it does By default the Record button is a toggle: press to start, press to stop. Push-to-Talk changes it to a dead man's switch: recording runs only while the button is held and stops the instant you release. Nothing can be left recording between studies, no half-toggled state, and the start/stop rhythm matches how many radiologists already dictate on legacy systems. How to turn it on / set it up Settings → Desktop Mode → Push-to-Talk Dead Man's Switch . It applies to the PowerMic/SpeechMike Record button and works with both Mosaic's built-in dictation and Custom STT Mode. Using it Squeeze, speak a finding, release. With Custom STT and Turbo Paste, text lands essentially as you release; the ensemble's slower voters finish their corrections in the moment after. Short bursts per press work well — the ensemble is tuned for exactly that dictation style, and the setup wizard even asks whether you dictate in short bursts. Tips & gotchas - MosaicTools uses a desired-state model for the held button, so a fast squeeze-release never wedges the recorder in the wrong state. - The speech-gating cost saver which pauses cloud streaming during silence applies only to hands-free auto-start dictation — under Push-to-Talk the held button itself defines the session, so there's no idle time to save. - If you prefer hands-free, the opposite workflow exists too: Auto-start on case open in Speech-to-Text settings begins recording when a study opens and stops on Sign Report. Pick one style; they compose poorly."},{"id":"radai","name":"RadAI Impression","brief":"Legacy integration with the RadAI impression generator — being retired; MTRec and Mosaic's own impression generation cover the ground.","category":"Recommendations","tags":"radai rad ai impression ai impression legacy","body":"What it does For workstations with RadAI installed, MosaicTools can drive it: the mappable RadAI Impression action generates and shows an AI impression, and Settings → Alerts → RadAI auto-insert on Process Report generates and inserts one automatically when you press Process Report. Why it's deprecated The integration is being retired. It only ever worked on workstations with the separate RadAI application installed, and the ground it covered is now handled inside MosaicTools and Mosaic itself — Mosaic generates the impression during Process Report, and MTRec adds guideline follow-up recommendations from verified tables with citations see the MTRec pages . If you have the auto-insert option enabled on a machine without RadAI, it does nothing; you can safely uncheck it. Expect the action and setting to disappear in a future release."},{"id":"rainbow-mode","name":"Rainbow Mode","brief":"Colors each impression point and its matching findings sentences the same color, so you can see at a glance that everything important made it into the impression.","category":"Report Display & Highlighting","tags":"rainbow correlation findings impression coloring highlight coverage appendix uterus","body":"What it does Rainbow Mode correlates your FINDINGS and IMPRESSION sections and color-codes the matches: impression point 1 and the findings sentences that support it get one color, point 2 and its findings another, and so on colors are stable per body-part category, so the liver is always the same hue . One glance tells you whether every significant finding is represented in the impression — and which impression points have no visible support. It works in two places: - The report popup — click the popup to cycle into Rainbow mode see the Report Popup page . - Live in the Mosaic report editor — with CDP enabled, three small buttons No Highlight / Regular / Rainbow sit at the bottom-right of the report editor. Pick Rainbow and the Final Report itself is colored, updating with a ~2-second debounce as you edit. How to turn it on / set it up Settings → Report Display → check Rainbow Mode findings-impression correlation for the popup. The in-editor buttons require Use CDP for Mosaic interaction Settings → Experimental . Using it After Process Report, scan the colors: matched pairs share a hue; anything uncolored in FINDINGS didn't correlate to an impression point. As of 5.5.1, any mention of the appendix or uterus is always highlighted — those structures are never in the default CT abdomen/pelvis templates, so any comment about them even \"normal appendix\" is new information worth a look. The template's boilerplate \"No evidence of appendicitis\" is intentionally not affected. Tips & gotchas - Since 5.5.1 your chosen highlight mode None / Regular / Rainbow persists across restarts and study changes — it no longer resets to Regular each launch. - Rainbow is a heuristic term-matcher, not an AI read — use it as a coverage check, not a guarantee."},{"id":"recomd","name":"RecoMD Integration","brief":"Legacy integration with the external RecoMD recommendation service — superseded by the built-in MTRec engine.","category":"Recommendations","tags":"recomd recommendations legacy best practice","body":"What it does RecoMD is an external best-practice recommendation service. MosaicTools can send it report data Settings → Alerts → RecoMD integration , with an Auto-send on Process Report sub-option and paste its recommendations back via two mappable actions, Trigger RecoMD and Paste RecoMD . Paste RecoMD deduplicates against your existing impression points, verifies the copy actually happened, and restores your mouse position. Why it's deprecated MTRec 5.3.0 is the in-house replacement: offline, instant, no external agent or window-scraping, with every recommendation drawn from verified guideline tables and closed with its citation. If you still have RecoMD wired to a button, the MTRec pages are where to start — enable the engine under Settings → MTRec and either use the MTRec Recommendations key or the automatic MTRec boxes after Process Report. The RecoMD integration remains functional for workstations that still run the service note the settings hint: it requires \"Wait for Impression\" to be OFF in RecoMD's own settings , but it receives no further development."},{"id":"report-popup","name":"Report Popup","brief":"An overlay that shows your report right after Process Report — click it to cycle from Changes highlighting to Rainbow correlation to Orphan (unmatched) findings.","category":"Report Display & Highlighting","tags":"report overlay changes highlight rainbow orphan findings transparency show report delete impression points","body":"What it does After Process Report finishes, MosaicTools pops up the full report as an overlay so you can review it without leaving the images. The popup has three display modes that you cycle through by clicking anywhere on it: 1. Changes — newly added text is highlighted in your chosen color, so you can instantly see what this Process Report pass actually changed. 2. Rainbow — each impression point and its matching findings sentences share a color see the Rainbow Mode page . 3. Orphan findings — shows only the findings that are not represented in the impression, to catch things you meant to carry down. How to turn it on / set it up Settings → Report Display: - Show Report overlay after Process Report — the main toggle. - Highlight report changes — enables the Changes mode, with a color picker and a transparency slider default light green at 30% . - Rainbow Mode findings-impression correlation and Unmatched Findings highlight findings missing from impression — add the other two click-cycle modes. - Transparent overlay see image through report — renders the popup with a semi-transparent background so the radiology image shows through; a Transparency slider controls how see-through it is. - Show delete buttons on impression points — adds trash icons next to numbered impression points; click one to remove that point, and the edited impression pastes back into Mosaic about a second later. You can also summon the same popup on demand with the Show Report action map it under Keys & Buttons . Tips & gotchas - The delete buttons on impression points require opaque mode — they are unavailable while the transparent overlay is on. - Only modes you've enabled appear in the click cycle; with everything on it goes Changes → Rainbow → Orphan and back."},{"id":"ruler-overlay","name":"Draggable Ruler Overlay","brief":"Puts a draggable, calibrated copy of InteleViewer's ruler on screen — drag it onto anything to measure, release and it snaps back home.","category":"Measurement & Imaging","tags":"ruler overlay measure drag scale bar InteleViewer calibrated","body":"What it does InteleViewer draws its scale bars at the edges of the viewport, which is exactly where the finding isn't. This feature captures both the vertical and horizontal rulers , re-renders them as transparent always-on-top overlays sitting on the real scale bars, and makes them draggable: grab one, drag it over the structure you want to measure, read the tick marks, release — it snaps back to its home position. How to turn it on / set it up Settings → Keys & Buttons → InteleViewer Buttons → check Show draggable ruler overlay . No action mapping needed; the overlays appear whenever InteleViewer's rulers are visible. Using it - The overlays sit invisibly on top of InteleViewer's own rulers until you need them — drag to measure, release to snap back. - The overlay watches the underlying pixels and recaptures itself automatically when the ruler changes new zoom level, new series, window/level change , so what you drag around is always the ruler that matches the current image scale. - The rulers hide promptly when you switch to another app or minimize InteleViewer, and reappear when you come back. Tips & gotchas - Because the overlay is a calibrated copy of the actual scale bar, it's only as current as the last recapture — after a zoom change give it a beat it re-checks about every 1.5 s before trusting a borderline measurement. - Works with both yellow and orange InteleViewer ruler colors. - Measurement text and annotations near the ruler are excluded from capture, so annotations don't get misread as tick marks. - For a lesion you want an actual number pasted for, Auto Measure is usually faster — the ruler overlay shines for quick \"is this about a centimeter?\" checks."},{"id":"settings-window","name":"Settings Window","brief":"One settings window for everything — sidebar navigation by topic, a live search box that filters every option, and full backup/restore of your configuration.","category":"Setup & Platform","tags":"settings configuration search sidebar backup restore tooltips","body":"What it does All of MosaicTools' configuration lives in a single dark-themed window with a sidebar of sections: Profile , Desktop Mode , Keys & Buttons , Text & Templates , Alerts , Report Display , Behavior , TBWU & Metrics , Speech-to-Text , Summarize Prior , MTRec , Experimental , and Reference . Nothing is buried in config files — every option is a labeled control with a tooltip. How to turn it on / set it up Open it by clicking the widget-bar title, right-click → Settings , or double-clicking the tray icon. Changes apply when you save; some like the diagnostics opt-in apply live. Using it - Search : the Search settings... box at the top filters sections and options as you type, including hidden alias terms \"autostart\", \"mic gain calibrator\" , so you don't need to know which tab something lives on. - 🆕 What's New top bar : opens the full scrollable version history — every release's notes, not just the latest. - Tooltips : hover any control for an explanation. Turn them off with Show tooltips throughout settings in Profile if you find them noisy. - Backup / Restore : right-click the widget bar → Backup Settings saves your entire configuration mappings, macros, pick lists, templates, STT setup to a .backup file; Restore Settings loads one and reloads the app. Settings are stored as JSON in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\MosaicTools\\ . Tips & gotchas - Back up before big experiments and before moving to a new workstation — restoring the backup on the new box carries everything over. - Sub-editors Macro Editor, Pick List Editor, Impression Fixer editor, Key Mappings, Button Studio open as their own windows from their sections and save independently. - If Settings won't open at all on a locked-down PC, it's usually the Windows RichEdit component failing to load — see the error dialog's guidance, or reinstall to a local non-synced folder."},{"id":"sign-warning","name":"Unprocessed-Sign Warning","brief":"Beeps and shows a red warning box when you press Sign while dictation is still sitting unprocessed in the transcript box — press Sign again to sign anyway, or Process Report to capture it first.","category":"Report Processing & QA","tags":"sign premature sign unprocessed transcript warning alt+f safety","body":"What it does The failure mode this prevents: you dictate a late addition, forget to re-run Process Report, and sign — the report goes out missing your last sentences. When you trigger Sign Report Alt+F in Mosaic , MosaicTools first checks whether the transcript box looks unprocessed. If it does, it beeps and shows a red, movable warning box instead of signing. It distinguishes two cases and words the box accordingly: dictation that was never processed \"may not be in the report\" , and dictation that changed after you processed — a late edit or a streaming correction that arrived after the fact \"Press Process Report again to capture it\" . How to turn it on / set it up Settings → Behavior → Warn before signing unprocessed transcript . Sign Report itself is mapped under Keys & Buttons . Using it The box never steals your keyboard — it's dismissed through your normal actions: - Press Sign again — signs anyway you've decided the transcript remainder doesn't matter . - Press Process Report — processes the outstanding dictation first; then sign. - Start dictating again — the box closes and gets out of your way. It's draggable and remembers its position between sessions. Tips & gotchas - MosaicTools' own inserts into the transcript a macro, a corrected clinical history don't count as unprocessed dictation — only text you actually dictated or edited triggers it, which keeps false warnings rare. - The two-press pattern means a determined double-tap of Sign still signs quickly; the warning costs you one keypress exactly when it might save an addendum. - The alert tone was made deliberately audible tuned in 5.1.x — if you sign and hear a beep, look before pressing again."},{"id":"speaker-gate","name":"Ambient Voice Filtering (Speaker Gate)","brief":"EXPERIMENTAL — a one-time voiceprint of your voice lets the ensemble drop words spoken by other people (background conversation, TV) instead of dictating them into your report.","category":"Dictation & Speech","tags":"speaker gate voice enrollment voiceprint ambient background voices tv experimental","body":"What it does If you read in a room with other people or a TV, stray speech can end up in your transcript. The speaker gate builds a one-time voiceprint of your voice and then, during dictation, drops words that clearly weren't spoken by you. It runs alongside the ensemble with no added latency and is deliberately conservative — it only removes speech that is confidently not-you; everything borderline passes through and the report-processing AI already filters most ambient leakage anyway . Be aware: this is experimental and not yet generally available. It is currently exercised only on the developer's own workstation and does not ship functional in regular releases. The page exists so you know it's coming and what it will look like. How to turn it on / set it up When available: Settings → Speech-to-Text → Ambient Voice Filtering experimental . Click Enroll my voice… — a dialog shows a short radiology passage to read aloud ~20–30 seconds of normal dictation cadence and builds the voiceprint. Then check Filter out other voices background people / TV . Using it Start in Shadow mode log only — do NOT remove words : the gate logs what it would drop but never touches your report. Only after the logs confirm it's catching the right things should the shadow switch come off. A Strictness slider trades aggressiveness against the risk of clipping your own words default 0.15, the safe end . Tips & gotchas - Zero risk while in shadow mode — that's the intended way to trial it. - Higher strictness removes more background speech but can clip your own quiet asides; move it up slowly. - Re-enroll if you change microphones dramatically."},{"id":"start-with-windows","name":"Start with Windows","brief":"Launch MosaicTools automatically every time you log in to Windows — one checkbox, no admin rights.","category":"Setup & Platform","tags":"startup autostart boot login launch run at startup","body":"What it does Registers MosaicTools in the Windows startup registry so it launches on login/boot. No more remembering to start it before the first study of the shift — the bar is just there when you sit down. How to turn it on / set it up Check Start MosaicTools automatically when Windows starts in Settings → Profile and save. It uses the per-user startup registry key, so no admin rights are needed and it only affects your Windows account. Using it Log in; MosaicTools starts with your session. That's the whole feature. Tips & gotchas - The checkbox reflects the registry's actual state , not a saved preference — if you disable MosaicTools under Task Manager → Startup apps or Windows Settings → Apps → Startup , the checkbox will correctly show unchecked next time you look. - Saving settings refreshes the stored exe path, so if you move MosaicTools.exe to a new folder, open Settings and hit Save once to update the startup entry. - The auto-updater's rename trick keeps the exe path stable across updates, so startup keeps working after every update without any action from you. - Combine with auto-update on startup for the zero-touch experience: log in, MosaicTools starts, checks for updates, and is current before your first study."},{"id":"stroke-detection","name":"Stroke Detection","brief":"Flags stroke studies with a purple alert the moment they open, and can create the Clario communication note for you — on a click, or automatically when you press Process Report.","category":"Worklist & Productivity","tags":"stroke cva alert purple border clario note auto-create clinical history keywords","body":"What it does Watches each study as it opens and shows a purple border alert on the notification box when it looks like a stroke case. From there it can handle the Clario communication note for you: click the alert to create it, or let MosaicTools create it automatically when you press Process Report. How to turn it on / set it up In Settings → Alerts , under Alert Triggers : 1. Check Stroke detection purple border . 2. Optional: Also use clinical history keywords — in addition to the study description, the clinical history is checked against a keyword list. Click Edit... to customize the keywords one per line . 3. Choose how the note gets created: - Click alert to create Clario note — clicking the purple alert creates the communication note. - Auto-create Clario note on Process Report — the note is created automatically when you process a stroke study's report. Using it Open a stroke study, see the purple border, read the case. If auto-create is on, pressing Process Report files the Clario note without any extra step, and the study lands in the critical-studies tracker. Tips & gotchas - The auto-note only fires on a manual Process Report since 5.5.1 — never on the automatic processing that runs shortly after a drafted study opens. You won't get a stroke note filed before you've even looked at the images. - If you already created a Negative Critical on the study documenting a negative stroke read , the automatic positive stroke note is suppressed. - Detection is keyword-based, so tune the keyword list to your practice's history phrasing \"code stroke\", \"CVA\", \"tPA\", etc. if you see misses or false alerts. - Requires the notification box scraping to be running Settings → Behavior → Scrape Mosaic ."},{"id":"stt-data-sharing","name":"Dictation Data Sharing (Opt-in)","brief":"Strictly opt-in sharing of de-identified dictation data to improve recognition accuracy — all PHI is stripped on your own machine before anything leaves it.","category":"Dictation & Speech","tags":"data sharing privacy de-identified phi corpus opt-in audio contribution","body":"What it does Recognition problems are fixed by looking at real dictations — which engine misheard what, and how the merge decided. Share de-identified dictation to improve accuracy contributes anonymized dictation data for exactly that tuning. The critical part is where de-identification happens: on your computer, before anything is shared . Names, dates, MRNs, accession numbers, and hospital/facility names are removed locally; nothing identifiable ever leaves your machine. Sharing is one-way push only and off by default. A second, narrower opt-in — Contribute ~30 min of de-identified dictation audio to improve engine recommendations — is only visible to testers who have arranged it with the developer. Audio can't be scrubbed the way text can, so each clip is checked on your machine first and any clip with a detected identifier is dropped and never uploaded; it stops automatically at about 30 minutes total. How to turn it on / set it up Settings → Speech-to-Text → Data Sharing . Both boxes default to off; check the first if you're willing to contribute. Nothing else to do. Using it Invisible in daily work. The practical benefit flows back to you: engine misfires seen in the shared data drive keyterm additions, merge fixes, and ensemble recommendations in subsequent releases. Tips & gotchas - Independent of this, ensemble mode keeps a local de-identified diagnostics log dictation log.jsonl , next to the main log that never leaves your machine unless you personally send it — right-click the bar → Show Log to find it. - You can switch either opt-in off at any time; it stops immediately. - What's shared is dictation/diagnostic data — never raw patient reports with identifiers."},{"id":"stt-ensemble","name":"STT Ensemble","brief":"Runs several speech engines on the same audio at once and merges them by word-level voting — engines correct each other, so the pasted text is better than any single engine alone.","category":"Dictation & Speech","tags":"ensemble voting anchor driver secondaries merge consensus back-patch accuracy","body":"What it does Every engine mishears differently. The ensemble streams your dictation to up to seven voting engines at once: a Driver the anchor — a streaming engine that produces the live transcript and up to six Secondaries that vote on it word by word. Where the driver is confident and the others agree, the word stands; where they disagree, consensus wins. Corrections happen two ways: live, as you dictate, and by back-patch — a slower engine's final result arriving seconds later is folded back into text already pasted, so late evidence still counts. There are also non-voting observer slots that run alongside purely for comparison and never touch your report currently a developer-only evaluation tool — regular installs won't see them . How to turn it on / set it up Settings → Speech-to-Text → Ensemble Mode → Enable Ensemble Mode . Pick the Driver and Secondary 1–6 engines each slot has a Test button to validate its saved key . The easiest path is the Quick Setup Wizard, which fills the slots with the current Recommended Ensemble from whatever engines you have keys for. Merge wait ms and Correct below % tune how long the merge waits for voters and the confidence threshold below which the driver gets corrected — the defaults are fine. Using it Nothing changes at the microphone. You'll occasionally see a word snap to a better one a beat after it pasted — that's a back-patch landing. Tips & gotchas - Every voting slot needs its own working paid API key except medASR, which is free and local. - Batch engines Voxtral, Soniox Async, gpt-4o-transcribe can't drive — the Driver must stream. - A guard prevents late corrections from re-inserting sentences already in your report."},{"id":"stt-providers","name":"Speech Engines (Providers)","brief":"The roster of speech engines MosaicTools can run — all cloud engines are paid on your own account; medASR is free and runs locally on your workstation.","category":"Dictation & Speech","tags":"providers engines deepgram assemblyai soniox speechmatics openai whisper voxtral chirp corti mai elevenlabs amazon medasr pricing api key","body":"What it does MosaicTools speaks to a dozen speech-recognition engines through one interface, so any of them can be your driver, a voting secondary, or a failover backup. The current roster: - Deepgram Nova-3 Medical — the recommended driver; medical-tuned streaming ~$0.0077/min, free starter credit - AssemblyAI U3 Pro — medical-domain streaming with large keyterm support - Speechmatics Medical — streaming, US/EU regions - Soniox streaming and Soniox v5 Async batch, same key - OpenAI Realtime Whisper streaming, ~$0.017/min and gpt-4o-transcribe batch back-fill - Mistral Voxtral batch - Google Chirp 3 — needs a Google Cloud service-account JSON - Microsoft MAI-Transcribe — needs an Azure AI Foundry resource key + endpoint - Corti Symphony — OAuth client credentials; enterprise/contract billing - ElevenLabs Scribe v2 — single key; watch for paid monthly plans - Amazon Transcribe Medical — AWS IAM credentials ~$0.075/min - MedASR — free , runs entirely on your workstation; downloads itself the first time you select it How to turn it on / set it up Pick an engine under Settings → Speech-to-Text → Provider and enter its credentials Get API Key opens the right signup page; Test key validates without sending audio . The Quick Setup Wizard does all of this for you, engine by engine. Tips & gotchas - Every cloud engine is paid, on your own account — mostly pay-per-use, so an idle key costs nothing. Exceptions: ElevenLabs sells monthly plans and Corti is contract-billed; cancel those if unused. - medASR is excellent on a wired microphone but degrades badly on Bluetooth headsets — keep it wired or drop it. - The multi-field engines Chirp, MAI have a \"Copy AI setup prompt\" button — paste it into an AI assistant for guided account setup."},{"id":"stt-setup-wizard","name":"Quick Setup Wizard","brief":"A guided, one-step-at-a-time wizard that creates the speech-engine accounts, captures and validates the API keys, and assembles the recommended ensemble for you.","category":"Dictation & Speech","tags":"wizard setup quick setup api keys accounts onboarding recommended ensemble","body":"What it does Setting up multi-engine dictation by hand means a half-dozen accounts, consoles, and key formats. The wizard turns that into a sequence of small steps: for each recommended engine it opens the signup page in your real browser steering you to \"Sign in with Google\" so there are no new passwords , then watches your clipboard — the moment you click Copy on the provider's key page, the wizard grabs the key, validates it with a real connection to the provider, stores it, and moves on. Nothing to paste. At the end it arranges everything you set up into the current Recommended Ensemble driver + voting secondaries in priority order . How to turn it on / set it up Settings → Speech-to-Text → ⚡ Quick Setup Wizard . It's safe to re-run any time; it remembers your previous answers. Using it Beyond the key steps, the wizard also: turns on the two prerequisites Custom dictation and Direct Mosaic access with one click if they're off; adds the free on-device medASR model; offers an optional Advanced section for Microsoft MAI-Transcribe and Google Chirp 3; offers a Backup engines step for failover resilience; and switches Mosaic's \"Auto Transcript Record\" off so Mosaic doesn't grab the microphone first. If the recommended line-up changes in a future update, you get a one-time tip to re-run it. Tips & gotchas - A rejected key blocks the Next button on purpose — the wizard tells you plainly whether the key is bad or it looks like a network problem. - On the finish step it lists any engine you set up that the recommended stack won't use, and whether leaving it idle is free pay-per-use or could keep charging you e.g. an ElevenLabs monthly plan to cancel . - Skip the Advanced engines freely — dictation works well without them."},{"id":"summarize-prior","name":"Summarize Prior","brief":"Opens the prior report from InteleViewer in a popup with a short AI summary on top — the gist of the prior in five seconds, full text below if you want it.","category":"Priors & Clinical History","tags":"prior summary AI Java Access Bridge OpenAI Gemini Claude popup gist","body":"What it does With InteleViewer active, Summarize Prior reads the prior report straight from InteleViewer's Search Tool via Java Access Bridge — no clipboard, no clicking , cleans up the formatting, and shows it in a popup with a short AI-generated summary at the top: 1–4 telegraphic bullets covering the positive findings, any follow-up the prior recommended, and — because your current study's clinical history is sent along as context — the pertinent negative that answers today's question. A negative prior comes back as a single line \"Prior shoulder XR negative\" , not a list of everything normal. The full formatted prior sits below the summary for when you need the detail. How to turn it on / set it up 1. Settings → Summarize Prior → check Enable AI summary of prior reports . 2. Pick a Model — the family picks the provider: gpt- → OpenAI, gemini- → Google, claude- → Anthropic. You can type a custom model name. 3. Enter the matching API key OpenAI key / Gemini key / Claude key — only one is needed . The OpenAI and Gemini keys are shared with Custom STT and Custom Process Report, so if you've entered one there it's already filled in. 4. Optionally set a Custom prompt to override the built-in summary instructions. 5. Map the Summarize Prior action under Keys & Buttons. These are paid cloud APIs — a summary costs a fraction of a cent, billed to your own key. Using it Open the prior in InteleViewer's Search Tool, trigger the action. The popup shows \"Summarizing…\" and fills in as the model responds; the header shows \"Prior: <study type \" with the study date. It's resizable drag the side edges , remembers its size and position, and closes on Esc or right-click — and automatically when you sign or discard the study. Tips & gotchas - Summaries are cached per study: dismiss the popup and re-summon the same prior or flip between a few priors on one patient and the saved summary reappears instantly with no second AI call. The cache clears on the next study. - The summary preserv"},{"id":"tbwu-counter","name":"TBWU Counter","brief":"Live TBWU productivity metrics on the widget bar — total, per-hour, pace, and goal tracking — pulled from your local RADCounter database, with optional injection of live TBWU values into the Clario worklist.","category":"Worklist & Productivity","tags":"tbwu rvu radcounter rvucounter productivity pace car goal metrics clario worklist","body":"What it does Reads your shift's TBWU data from the local RADCounter RVUCounter SQLite database and shows it live on the MosaicTools bar. You choose which metrics appear: Total , TBWU/h , This Hour , Prev Hour , Est Total , TBWU/Study , Avg/h , and Pace +/- e.g. \"+2.1 ahead\" . It can also replace Clario's broken TBWU column with live values. How to turn it on / set it up 1. Open Settings → TBWU & Metrics . 2. Under Database , click Find to auto-detect the RADCounter database, or ... to browse to it. RADCounter must be installed for any of this to work. 3. Under Display Metrics , check the metrics you want on the bar. With 3+ metrics selected, the 3+ layout: dropdown controls presentation: Horizontal , Vertical Stack , Hover Popup , or Carousel cycles every 4s . 4. Optional: check Goal: and set a per-hour target — TBWU/h turns red on the bar when you fall below it. 5. Optional: check Show full pace car information under Pace Car — the display alternates between your TBWU metrics and a pace comparison every few seconds Alternate every N sec , 3–30 . 6. Optional: check Inject TBWU values into Clario worklist under Clario Integration to overwrite Clario's TBWU display with live RADCounter values requires Clario CDP . Using it The bar shows your selected metrics next to the \"TBWU\" label and updates continuously through the shift. In Hover Popup or Vertical Stack layouts, secondary metrics live in a small popup/drawer that matches the bar styling. Pace +/- tells you at a glance whether you're ahead of or behind your goal pace. Tips & gotchas - User-facing branding is TBWU everywhere the RVU→TBWU rename ; internally and on disk the tool still reads RVUCounter's rvu data.db . - Est Total projects your end-of-shift total from your current rate — useful early in a shift, noisy in the first hour. - If metrics stop updating mid-shift, check that RADCounter is still running and has an active shift; MosaicTools only reads the database, it doesn't count studies itself."},{"id":"telemetry","name":"Diagnostics & Data Sharing","brief":"Two opt-in sharing switches — app diagnostics and de-identified dictation — that let bugs get fixed remotely. Everything is scrubbed on your machine before upload, and nothing identifiable ever leaves it.","category":"Setup & Platform","tags":"telemetry diagnostics privacy de-identification phi hipaa data sharing opt-in","body":"What it does MosaicTools has two separate, opt-in, default-off sharing switches: 1. Share app diagnostics to help troubleshoot issues Settings → Profile — a one-way stream of errors, crashes, config, and version info, so problems can be diagnosed without asking you to dig up log files. It contains no patient info, no report text, and no record of what or how much you read — by design, it cannot be a productivity tracker. 2. Share de-identified dictation to improve accuracy Settings → Speech-to-Text → Data Sharing — contributes anonymized dictation data used to tune the speech-recognition ensemble for everyone. What leaves the machine — and what never does De-identification happens on your computer, before anything is written or uploaded . Every free-text string passes through the scrubber first: patient and clinician names, MRNs, accession numbers, dates of service, phone numbers, emails, and even hospital/facility names are removed or replaced. Accession numbers never appear at all — only a salted one-way pseudonym, which cannot be reversed into a PACS lookup. Study descriptions are collapsed to coarse categories. What's kept is exactly the clinical content needed to judge transcription accuracy measurements, anatomy, findings , because corrupting those would defeat the purpose. Upload is push-only to the developer's private storage — nothing on your machine can be browsed or pulled remotely; only what the scrubbed stream sends goes out. How to turn it on / set it up Check either box or both and save; they apply immediately. Uncheck any time to stop sharing. Why say yes Nearly every remote fix in the changelog — dead STT providers, update failures on locked-down PCs, the blank-panel scrape bug — was found and verified through these streams, usually before the affected radiologist filed a report. Honest caveat: no automated scrubber is theoretically perfect against free text, which is why the scrubber is conservative, audited, and continuously improved — and why the"},{"id":"template-correction","name":"Template Mismatch & Auto-Correct","brief":"A red-border alert when the loaded report template doesn't match the study type — with an optional attempt to fix it automatically by re-typing the study description into Mosaic.","category":"Report Processing & QA","tags":"template wrong template study type mismatch auto correct alerts","body":"What it does Dictating a CT abdomen into a chest-radiograph template is a classic wrong-template miss. MosaicTools compares the report template Mosaic loaded against the study description, and when they disagree it puts a red border alert on the notification box. Optionally, it goes one step further and tries to repair it: it types the study description into Mosaic's Study field so Mosaic swaps in the right template. How to turn it on / set it up Settings → Alerts → Alert Triggers → Template mismatch turns on the alert. The sub-option Attempt to correct template enables the automatic fix. Both live under the same tab as the other alert triggers, and require the notification box to be enabled. Using it Open a study; if the template is wrong you'll see the red border immediately, before you've dictated anything into the wrong skeleton. With auto-correct on, most mismatches fix themselves without you touching anything — the study description lands in the Study field and Mosaic reloads the matching template. Tips & gotchas - Matching is tolerant of combined and protocol studies: CT+CTA combinations and perfusion protocols are handled, and a \"CT BRAIN PERFUSION CTA HEAD NECK\" won't false-flag against \"CTA Head and Neck with Perfusion\". - Auto-correct types into Mosaic — if you're mid-keystroke elsewhere it waits its turn through the normal action queue, but expect a brief flicker in the Study field when it fires. - If a template persistently mismatches for a study description your site words unusually, leave the alert on but auto-correct off and fix those by hand."},{"id":"transcript-comparison","name":"Transcript Comparison","brief":"A line-by-line view of the merged ensemble transcript with each engine's raw transcript underneath it — see exactly who heard what.","category":"Dictation & Speech","tags":"comparison side-by-side per-provider transcripts diff ensemble vs engine","body":"What it does When a dictated phrase comes out wrong, the first question is: did every engine mishear it, or did the merge pick the wrong voter? The transcript comparison popup answers that at a glance. It groups the dictation line by line — each ensemble merged line shown with the corresponding line from the driver and each secondary directly underneath, in a monospace font with color-coded engine prefixes. Differences between what an engine heard and what the ensemble shipped jump out immediately. How to turn it on / set it up It's opened from the live ensemble metrics popup Settings → Speech-to-Text → Ensemble Mode → Show live metrics popup , which has a control to pop out the full comparison for the current or last dictation. No separate setting. Using it Typical use is forensic: you notice \"anasarca\" pasted as something odd, open the comparison, and see that two engines heard it correctly while the driver garbled it — or that every engine garbled it, meaning the audio itself mic, distance, competing noise is the problem. The window is resizable, scrollable, and remembers its size and position between uses. Tips & gotchas - The columns follow your configured slots — whatever engine you've chosen as driver anchors the view. - If one engine's line is consistently empty, that engine isn't producing finals — check the ensemble health drawer for the reason. - This shows raw per-engine text before text processing; small formatting differences from the pasted report units, punctuation are expected and not errors."},{"id":"turbo-paste","name":"Turbo Paste","brief":"Near-instant pasting of dictated text that quietly refines itself in place as the full ensemble result arrives.","category":"Dictation & Speech","tags":"stt speed paste latency instant refine text coloring","body":"What it does Normally the ensemble waits a beat the merge wait so every engine can vote before text is pasted. Turbo Paste collapses that wait: the first transcript appears in your report almost instantly, and the other engines' corrections catch up a moment later, editing the text in place . You get single-engine speed with full-ensemble accuracy — the end state of the text is the same, it just arrives sooner. How to turn it on / set it up Settings → Speech-to-Text → Text Processing → ⚡ Turbo Paste . It's on by default when Custom STT is enabled. Using it Dictate normally. Text lands as fast as the driver engine can produce it; occasionally you'll see a word or phrase snap to a corrected version a second or two later as the slower voters and back-patch corrections land. If you also enable Text coloring the checkbox right below it , dictated words are tinted by ensemble confidence — amber for uncertain, orange for low confidence, magenta for a flagged non-word — so your eye is drawn to exactly the spots most likely to change or need a glance. The two settings are independent: you can have instant paste without coloring, or coloring without Turbo Paste. Tips & gotchas - If watching text mutate bothers you, turn Turbo Paste off — you'll get the fully merged result in one shot, just slightly later. - Corrections only ever replace what the ensemble concluded was misheard; a guard prevents late results from duplicating sentences already in the report. - Turbo Paste changes when text appears, not what gets processed — Process Report sees the same final transcript either way."},{"id":"visual-enhancements","name":"Visual Report Enhancements","brief":"Restyles the Mosaic report editor via CDP — Carolina-blue change tracking, inline subsection headers, and a size hierarchy that makes the report easier to scan.","category":"Report Display & Highlighting","tags":"visual styling headers paragraph flow carolina blue change tracking CDP editor","body":"What it does With CDP access to Mosaic's report editor, MosaicTools can restyle the Final Report as you work: - Carolina blue change-tracking highlight — a calmer, more readable color for changed text than the default. - Inline subsection headers — section headers flow inline with their paragraph instead of consuming their own line, so more report fits on screen and the FINDINGS section reads like prose grouped by organ. - Slightly smaller content text — headers stay prominent while body text steps down a notch, giving the report a visual hierarchy instead of a wall of same-size text. Nothing about the report's actual content changes — this is styling applied inside the editor, and the signed report is unaffected. How to turn it on / set it up 1. Settings → Experimental → check Use CDP for Mosaic interaction a Mosaic restart is needed on first enable . 2. Then check Visual report enhancements underneath it. Using it Once on, the styling applies automatically whenever a report is open. It re-applies itself after Mosaic re-renders, so it survives study changes and page reloads. Tips & gotchas - This lives under Experimental for a reason: it restyles a UI MosaicTools doesn't own, so a Mosaic update can occasionally change how it looks until MosaicTools catches up. - Pairs naturally with Independent column scrolling and the in-editor highlight modes No Highlight / Regular / Rainbow — all part of the same CDP toolbox. - Related nearby toggles in the same settings group: Hide report drag/delete handles removes the drag-to-reorder and delete icons on paragraphs and Auto-scroll to cursor during dictation ."},{"id":"widget-bar","name":"Widget Bar","brief":"The small always-on-top MosaicTools bar — TBWU metrics, critical-note count, STT health light, and connectivity dots at a glance, with everything else one right-click away.","category":"Setup & Platform","tags":"bar main window toolbar tray toast badge health light","body":"What it does The bar is MosaicTools' home: a compact, always-on-top strip you park at a screen edge. Left to right: - ⋮ drag handle — grab here to move the bar position is remembered . - Ensemble health dot — the STT traffic light green/yellow/red ; click for the per-provider drawer. - ⚠ critical count — red badge showing critical notes placed this session; click for the list. - Title — click to open Settings. - TBWU metrics — your selected productivity numbers. - Connectivity dots — a 2×2 grid of server-status dots when the Network Monitor is enabled ; click for details. Toast notifications stack at the bottom-right of the screen for action feedback, warnings, and update news. How to turn it on / set it up Nothing — it appears when MosaicTools starts unless you launch in headless mode . Individual indicators appear as their features are enabled TBWU metrics, connectivity dots, critical tracking, custom STT . Using it Right-click the bar for the menu: - Settings — the settings window also: click the title . - Show Log — opens the log folder with the log files selected, for sending in a bug report. - Reload — restarts the app in place. - Backup Settings / Restore Settings — save or restore your entire configuration as a .backup file. - Debug — copy diagnostic snapshots template, clinical history, Get Prior, Critical Findings, alerts to the clipboard. - Exit . A system-tray icon is always present too, with Settings / Show Log / Exit; double-click it to open Settings. Tips & gotchas - If the bar ends up on a disconnected monitor, Reload brings it back to a visible screen. - The health dot is split-colored during an STT failover: left half shows the original ensemble's health, right half the currently active stack. - Take a settings backup before experimenting — Restore + automatic reload gets you back in seconds."}];